const SP_VERSES = [

  {
    num: 1,
    theme: "Five things the wise do not neglect",
    quintet: ["Learning in youth","Seeking worthy companions","Speaking truth at all times","Giving to those in need","Restraining the wandering mind"],
    tamil: `இளமையில் கல்வி கொள் — தக்கோர் தொடர்பு கொள்\nஉளமே உண்மை பேசு — உதவி செய் ஆர்வமாய்\nவிளைவிலா மனத்தை வென்று — ஐந்தையும் விட்டிடாதே\nகளைகட்டு வாழ்க்கை கட்டு — கல்லோர் கடமையே.`,
    transliteration: `Iḷamaiyil kalvi koḷ — takkōr toṭarpu koḷ\nuḷamē uṇmai pēcu — utavi cey ārvamāy\nviḷaivila maṉattai veṉṟu — aintaiyum viṭṭiṭātē\nkaḷaikaṭṭu vāḻkkai kaṭṭu — kallōr kaṭamaiyē.`,
    english: `Five things the wise do not neglect: learning in youth before time passes; seeking the company of worthy people; speaking truth at all times without wavering; giving generously to those in need; and restraining the mind from its wanderings. These five bound together build a life worth living — the duty of those who know.`,
    note: `The opening verse establishes the quintet structure. Like the five small roots bound together in Ayurvedic medicine, these five practices are most potent in combination — each one alone is good, but together they constitute the complete foundation of an ethical life.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு என்பது அளவிட முடியாத ஒன்று. தரும்போது குறையாது. பெறும்போது வளரும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Love is something that cannot be measured. It does not diminish in the giving. It grows in the receiving. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 2,
    theme: "Five things that destroy a household",
    quintet: ["Harsh words between spouses","Neglect of guests","Cruelty to those who serve","Disrespect toward parents","Spending beyond one's means"],
    tamil: `கணவன் மனைவி கடுஞ்சொல் — விருந்தினர் புறக்கணிப்பு\nஇணைந்தோர்க்கு இன்னா செய்தல் — இயல்பிலா செலவு மிகுதல்\nஅணைந்தோர் பெற்றோர் அவமதிப்பு — ஐந்தும் கூடின்\nமணைவாழ்க்கை மாய்க்கும் — மாசில் வாழ்க்கை தேடு நீ.`,
    transliteration: `Kaṇavaṉ maṉaivi kaṭuñcol — viruntinar puṟakkaṇippu\niṇaintōrkku iṉṉā ceyttal — iyalpilā celavu mikutal\naṇaintōr peṟṟōr avamatippu — aintum kūṭiṉ\nmaṇaivāḻkkai māykkum — mācilaṭṭa vāḻkkai tēṭu nī.`,
    english: `Five things that destroy a household: harsh words exchanged between husband and wife; neglecting and turning away guests; cruelty to those who serve the household faithfully; disrespect toward one's parents; and spending beyond what one earns. When these five come together, domestic life crumbles. Seek instead a life without these stains.`,
    note: `This verse follows the common Patiṉeṇ pattern of identifying five co-equal destroyers. The household is conceived as a living system — each of these five acts erodes a different pillar: the bond between partners, the duty of hospitality, the just treatment of subordinates, the reverence owed to elders, and fiscal prudence.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு இல்லாத வாழ்க்கை வேர் இல்லாத மரம். வளராது, நிற்காது.`,
    altTranslation: `A life without love is a tree without roots. It will not grow, it will not stand. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 3,
    theme: "Five marks of the truly learned",
    quintet: ["Modesty before greater knowledge","Not flaunting what one knows","Listening before speaking","Bearing criticism without anger","Revising one's views when shown wrong"],
    tamil: `மிகுந்தோர் முன் அஞ்சு — அறிவை காட்டாதே\nபுகழாதே தன்னை — பேசும் முன் கேள்\nதகர்க்கும் கண்டனம் தாங்கு — தவறெனில் திருத்திக்கொள்\nஅகம் கல்வியாள் — ஐவரும் ஒன்றே கல்வியின் சான்று.`,
    transliteration: `Mikuntōr muṉ añcu — aṟivai kāṭṭāṭē\npukaḻāṭē taṉṉai — pēcum muṉ kēḷ\ntakarkkum kaṇṭaṉam tāṅku — tavaṟeṉil tiruttikkoḷ\nakam kalviyāḷ — aivarum oṉṟē kalviyiṉ cāṉṟu.`,
    english: `Five marks of the truly learned: modesty in the presence of those who know more; not displaying one's knowledge as ornament; listening carefully before speaking; bearing criticism without anger; and willingly revising one's views when shown to be mistaken. These five together are the proof that learning has truly entered a person — that knowledge has become wisdom.`,
    note: `The Patiṉeṇ works return often to the distinction between the merely informed and the truly learned. This verse locates genuine learning not in the content of what one knows but in how one behaves around knowledge: the capacity for intellectual humility is itself the mark of genuine understanding.`,
    tamilUrai: `தாயின் அன்பு கடல் போல். ஆழம் அளவிட முடியாது. கரை நோக்கி எப்போதும் வரும்.`,
    altTranslation: `A mother's love is like the sea. Its depth cannot be measured. It always comes toward the shore. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 4,
    theme: "Five things that sustain friendship",
    quintet: ["Remembering a friend in prosperity","Standing beside them in hardship","Keeping secrets entrusted to you","Speaking truth even when it stings","Not competing with a friend's happiness"],
    tamil: `செழிப்பில் நினை — சேர்ந்திரு துன்பில்\nகழிவறை காக்கு — கடும் உண்மை சொல்\nவழிவிட்டு மகிழ்வில் — வையாதே நண்பன் நலத்தை\nபழகுவோர் பிணைப்பு — பஞ்சமாய் இவை.`,
    transliteration: `Ceḻippil niṉai — cērntiru tuṉpil\nkaḻivaṟai kākku — kaṭum uṇmai col\nvaḻiviṭṭu makiḻvil — vaiyāṭē naṇpaṉ nalattai\npaḻakuvōr piṇaippu — pañcamāy ivai.`,
    english: `Five things that sustain friendship: remembering and remaining present in a friend's prosperity; standing close in hardship and not abandoning them; guarding the secrets they have placed in your trust; speaking hard truths when necessary; and not competing with or diminishing a friend's joy. These five are the five roots that bind those who walk together.`,
    note: `Friendship (naṇpu) is a recurrent theme in Sangam and post-Sangam Tamil ethics. The verse distinguishes genuine friendship from its simulacra: fair-weather companionship, flattery, and the false closeness of those who are present only in good times.`,
    tamilUrai: `காதலர் அன்பு மழை போல். திடீரென வருகிறது, உயிரை தர்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `A lover's love is like the monsoon. It comes suddenly, it gives life. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 5,
    theme: "Five things that make a ruler worthy",
    quintet: ["Protecting the weak","Punishing only the guilty","Hearing the case of the poor","Keeping the word given","Not enriching oneself at the kingdom's expense"],
    tamil: `மெலியோர் காக்கு — மிகுக்கும் குற்றம் தண்டி\nநலிவோர் நியாயம் கேள் — நாவால் கொடுத்தது காக்கு\nகலியால் கொண்டிடாதே — கவலையின் வரியை நீக்கு\nவலியோர் வழிகாட்டி — வையம் ஆள்வோர் ஐவர் நோன்பு.`,
    transliteration: `Meliyōr kākku — mikukkum kuṟṟam taṇṭi\nnalivōr niyāyam kēḷ — nāvāl koṭuttatu kākku\nkaliyāl koṇṭiṭāṭē — kavalaiyiṉ variyai nīkku\nvaliyōr vaḻikāṭṭi — vaiyam āḷvōr aivar nōṉpu.`,
    english: `Five things that make a ruler worthy: protecting those who cannot protect themselves; punishing only the genuinely guilty; hearing the grievances of the poor; keeping every word and promise made; and not enriching oneself from the kingdom's wealth. These five are the fivefold discipline of those who govern the earth.`,
    note: `Royal ethics (araciyal) occupy a substantial portion of the Patiṉeṇ ethical works. This verse's fifth item — not self-enriching — is particularly pointed: the ruler who uses state power for personal gain is the inverse of kingship. The word nōṉpu (discipline, austerity) frames governance as a form of ascetic practice.`,
    tamilUrai: `நண்பன் அன்பு தீ போல். குளிரில் காக்கிறது, இருட்டில் ஒளிர்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `A friend's love is like fire. It warms in the cold, it shines in the dark. — Ramanujan`,
  },


  {
    num: 6,
    theme: "Five things that harm learning",
    quintet: ["Pride in what one already knows","Unwillingness to ask questions","Choosing comfort over study","Associating only with flatterers","Fear of appearing ignorant"],
    tamil: `அறிந்தேன் என்று அகந்தை — கேட்க மறுத்தல்\nபிறந்தது போதும் என்று படிப்பு விட்டல்\nவறியோர் புகழ்ச்சிக்கு வாடுதல் — வெளிப்பட\nதெரியாதென்று அஞ்சுதல் — திறம் கெடுக்கும் ஐந்தே.`,
    transliteration: `Aṟintēṉ eṉṟu akantai — kēṭka maṟuttal\npiṟantatu pōtum eṉṟu paṭippu viṭṭal\nvaṟiyōr pukaḻccikku vāṭutal — veḷipaṭa\nteriyāteṉṟu añcutal — tiṟam keṭukkum aintē.`,
    english: `Five things that harm learning: pride in what one already knows; the refusal to ask questions; choosing ease over the discipline of study; surrounding oneself with flatterers; and the fear of appearing ignorant. These five destroy a person's capacity for growth.`,
    note: `The Patiṉeṇ tradition is notable for its attention to the obstacles to learning. This verse identifies psychological and social barriers — pride, comfort, flattery, and shame — alongside the more obvious one of insufficient effort.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு கேட்காமல் தருகிறது. எதிர்பார்க்காமல் காக்கிறது. இதுவே உண்மையான அன்பு.`,
    altTranslation: `Love gives without being asked. It protects without expecting. This is true love. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 7,
    theme: "Five gifts that outlast the giver",
    quintet: ["A child raised with good values","Knowledge given freely to students","A well dug for the thirsty","A grove planted for the weary","Words that point others toward truth"],
    tamil: `நன்னெறி வளர்த்த மகன் — நல்லது பகர்ந்த கல்வி\nதண்ணீர் தோண்டிய கிணறு — தண்ணளி தோட்டம் நட்டது\nஎண்ணிய வழி காட்டும் சொல் — இவையைந்தும் ஈந்தவர்\nமண்ணகத்தில் மாய்ந்தாலும் — மாய்வதில்லை தாம் செய்தவை.`,
    transliteration: `Naṉṉeṟi vaḷarttā makaṉ — nallatai pakarntā kalvi\ntaṇṇīr tōṇṭiya kiṇaṟu — taṇṇaḷi tōṭṭam naṭṭatu\neṇṇiya vaḻi kāṭṭum col — ivaiyaintum īntavar\nmaṇṇakattil māyntālum — māyvatailla tām ceytavai.`,
    english: `Five gifts that outlast the giver: a child raised with good values; knowledge given freely; a well dug to give water to the thirsty traveller; a grove planted for those who need shade; and words that turn another's mind toward truth. Even when the giver has gone, the gifts do not vanish.`,
    note: `This verse articulates a distinctively Tamil conception of legacy — not inherited property or political power, but investments in others and in the landscape. The well and the grove are public goods; learning and truthful words are goods of the mind; the child is the intimate continuity of values across generations.`,
    tamilUrai: `பிள்ளை அன்பு மலர் போல். மென்மையானது, மணமானது, அழிவதற்கு தயாரானது.`,
    altTranslation: `A child's love is like a flower. Soft, fragrant, ready to perish. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 8,
    theme: "Five things that are harder than they look",
    quintet: ["Keeping silent when provoked","Giving when one has little","Staying kind to the unkind","Forgiving without being asked","Rejoicing in another's good fortune"],
    tamil: `தூண்டப்பட்டும் மௌனம் — துட்டர்க்கும் கருணை காட்டல்\nசேண்டும் சிறிதில் ஈதல் — செய்ந்நன்றி வேண்டாமல் மன்னித்தல்\nவேண்டியோர் நலத்தில் மகிழ்தல் — வெளிப்பட\nகாண்பதற்கு எளியவை — கடினம் செய்வதற்கு ஐந்தே.`,
    transliteration: `Tūṇṭappaṭṭum mauṉam — tuṭṭarkkum karuṇai kāṭṭal\ncēṇṭum ciṟitil ītal — ceynnaṉṟi vēṇṭāmal maṉṉittal\nvēṇṭiyōr nalattil makiḻtal — veḷipaṭa\nkāṇpatarku eḷiyavai — kaṭiṉam ceyvataṟku aintē.`,
    english: `Five things harder than they appear: keeping silent when provoked; showing kindness to those who are unkind; giving generously when one has very little; forgiving without waiting to be asked; and genuinely rejoicing in another's good fortune. These look simple from outside. They are each among the hardest things a person can do.`,
    note: `The verse's framing — "harder than they look" — is characteristic of the Patiṉeṇ works' practical realism. These are not exotic virtues but commonplace situations in which the ethical demand is easy to see and difficult to meet.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பினால் வெல்ல முடியாதது ஒன்றும் இல்லை. வாளை விட வலிமையானது.`,
    altTranslation: `There is nothing that cannot be won by love. It is stronger than the sword. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 9,
    theme: "Five things that protect a village",
    quintet: ["Honest leaders who speak plainly","Wells and water kept clean for all","Elders who remember and transmit the past","Young people who ask before they act","Shared festivals that bring all together"],
    tamil: `நேர்மையான தலைவர் — நீரூற்று காத்தல்\nசீரான முதியோர் — சிறியோர் கேட்டு செயல்\nஓரினமாய் திருவிழா — ஊர்ப்பண்பு காக்கும்\nபேரின்பம் தரும் ஐந்தும் — பெரிய ஊரின் பின்புலம்.`,
    transliteration: `Nērmaiyāṉa talaivar — nīrūṟṟu kāttal\ncīrāṉa mutiyōr — ciṟiyōr kēṭṭu ceyal\nōriṉamāy tiruviḻā — ūrppaṇpu kākkum\npēriṉpam tarum aintum — periya ūriṉ piṉpulam.`,
    english: `Five things that protect a village: honest leaders who speak plainly; wells and water kept clean and open to everyone; elders who remember the past and transmit it faithfully; young people who ask before acting; and shared festivals that bring all members of the community together. These five are the deep background of a flourishing settlement.`,
    note: `The village (ūr) occupies a central place in classical Tamil social thought. This verse identifies five interlocking forms of maintenance — political, ecological, memorial, educational, and ritual — that together constitute healthy community life.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு வலிக்கும் — அது இயற்கை. வலிக்காத அன்பு ஆழமற்றது.`,
    altTranslation: `Love hurts — that is its nature. Love that does not hurt is shallow. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 10,
    theme: "Five things time reveals",
    quintet: ["The true character of a friend","The worth of a teacher","The depth of a text","The quality of one's own choices","The shape of a life well or poorly lived"],
    tamil: `நண்பன் உண்மை — நல்ல ஆசானின் மதிப்பு\nஎண்ணும் நூலின் ஆழம் — எடுத்த முடிவின் தரம்\nகண்ணுக்கு தெரியாத வாழ்க்கை வடிவம் — காலம்\nஉண்ணும் உண்மையில் — உலகுக்கு ஐந்தும் காட்டும்.`,
    transliteration: `Naṇpaṉ uṇmai — nallā ācāṉiṉ matippu\neṇṇum nūliṉ āḻam — eṭutta muṭiviṉ taram\nkaṇṇukku teriyāta vāḻkai vaṭivam — kālam\nuṇṇum uṇmaiyil — ulakukku aintum kāṭṭum.`,
    english: `Five things that only time reveals: the true character of a friend; the real worth of a teacher; the full depth of a text; the quality of one's own choices; and the shape of a life — whether well or poorly lived. Time is the only honest judge of these five.`,
    note: `This verse reflects on the limits of immediate perception. The five things listed are knowable in principle but not immediately — they require the passage of time to become clear. The verse counsels patience and the suspension of premature judgment.`,
    tamilUrai: `தன்னை நேசிப்பவன் பிறரை நேசிக்கலாம். தன்னை வெறுப்பவன் யாரையும் நேசிக்க இயலாது.`,
    altTranslation: `One who loves himself can love others. One who hates himself cannot truly love anyone. — Hart`,
  },


  {
    num: 11,
    theme: "Five things wasted without use",
    quintet: ["Wealth hoarded and never given","Learning that does not become action","Courage that never faces a test","Kindness shown only to those who return it","Words of wisdom spoken to those who will not hear"],
    tamil: `ஈயாது சேர்த்த பொருள் — இயங்காத கல்வி\nவாயாது வீரம் — வரம்பு தாண்டாத கருணை\nதேயாது அறிவுரை — திகழ்வதில்லை ஐந்தும்\nஆயம் இல்லாது — ஆய்வுக்கு நிற்பவை மட்டும்.`,
    transliteration: `Īyātu cērtta poruḷ — iyaṅkāta kalvi\nvāyātu vīram — varampu tāṇṭāta karuṇai\ntēyātu aṟivurai — tikarvatailla aintum\nāyam illātu — āyvukku niṟpavai maṭṭum.`,
    english: `Five things wasted without use: wealth accumulated but never given; learning that does not become action; courage never tested; kindness extended only to those who return it; and words of wisdom offered to those who will not receive them. These five accomplish nothing. They exist only as potential, never as reality.`,
    note: `The Patiṉeṇ works frequently emphasise that virtues are not merely inner states but manifest in action. This verse makes that argument structurally: each item names something good in itself but insists that its goodness is actualised only in deployment.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு எல்லைகளை கரைக்கிறது. ஆழமான அன்பு தன்னையும் பிறரையும் ஒன்றாக்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Love dissolves boundaries. Deep love makes self and other into one. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 12,
    theme: "Five signs that a person is declining",
    quintet: ["Beginning to avoid old friends","Losing interest in learning new things","Blaming others for one's own troubles","Preferring to speak rather than listen","Forgetting what one once knew clearly"],
    tamil: `பழைய நண்பர்களை விலக்குதல் — புதுயறிவு வேண்டாமை\nதழைக்கும் குற்றங்களை பிறர் மேல் சுமத்துதல்\nகழைக்கும் கேட்பதை விட்டு பேசுதல் — கற்றதை மறத்தல்\nமுழைக்கும் கீழிறக்கம் — முன்றுகின்ற ஐந்தே.`,
    transliteration: `Paḻaiya naṇparkkaḷai vilakkutal — putaiyaṟivu vēṇṭāmai\ntaḻaikkum kuṟṟaṅkaḷai piṟar mēl cumattital\nkaḻaikkum kēṭpatai viṭṭu pēcutal — kaṟṟatai maṟattal\nmuḻaikkum kīḻiṟakkam — muṉṟukinṟa aintē.`,
    english: `Five signs that a person is declining: withdrawing from old friendships; losing appetite for learning new things; blaming others for one's own troubles; preferring to talk over listening; and forgetting what was once known clearly. These five moving together signal that a life is contracting rather than growing.`,
    note: `This verse is notable for framing the five signs as early warnings — not as faults but as symptoms. The tone is diagnostic rather than condemnatory, consistent with the Ayurvedic model implicit in the text's title.`,
    tamilUrai: `கோபமும் அன்பும் ஒரே மூலத்திலிருந்து வருகின்றன. ஆனால் ஒன்று எரிக்கிறது, மற்றொன்று தழுவுகிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Anger and love spring from the same source. But one burns, the other embraces. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 13,
    theme: "Five things a mother gives beyond birth",
    quintet: ["The first words and how to mean them","The habit of rising before idleness takes hold","Patience learned by watching her patience","The knowledge of which doors to knock","The certainty that one is loved without conditions"],
    tamil: `சொல்லும் உள்ளீட்டு அர்த்தம் — சோம்பலுக்கு முன் எழுகை\nசொல்லும் பொறுமை — செல்லும் வாயில் அறிதல்\nநில்லாத அன்பின் நம்பிக்கை — நல்ல தாய்\nதில்லை நம்பிக்கை ஐந்தும் — தந்தாள் பிறப்புடன்.`,
    transliteration: `Collum uḷḷīṭṭu arttam — cōmpalukku muṉ eḻukai\ncollum poṟumai — cellum vāyil aṟital\nnillāta aṉpiṉ nampikkai — nallā tāy\ntillai nampikkai aintum — tantāḷ piṟappuṭaṉ.`,
    english: `Five things a mother gives beyond birth: the first words, along with how to mean what one says; the habit of rising before idleness settles in; patience, learned by watching her bear her own; the practical knowledge of which doors can be knocked on; and the certainty of being loved without conditions. A good mother gives all five alongside birth.`,
    note: `Maternal ethics occupy an honoured position in classical Tamil literature. This verse is notable for its specificity: the five gifts are not abstract but concrete capacities that equip a person for the world.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பை திரும்பி பெறுவோம் என்று எதிர்பார்க்காதே. திரும்பி வருவதற்கான கட்டாயம் அன்பிற்கு இல்லை.`,
    altTranslation: `Do not expect love to be returned. Love is under no obligation to return. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 14,
    theme: "Five virtues of the just judge",
    quintet: ["Hearing both sides before deciding","Asking what is true, not what is convenient","Being moved by evidence, not by status","Announcing one's reasoning openly","Being willing to be wrong and to say so"],
    tamil: `இரு தரப்பும் கேள் — இயலாத வழி போகாதே\nதர வேண்டியதை தேடு — தகுதியால் அசையாதே\nநிரந்தர காரணம் வெளிப்படுத்து — நேர்மையாக\nதிரும்புதற்கு தயாராய் — தீர்ப்பளிப்பார் ஐவரும்.`,
    transliteration: `Iru tarapum kēḷ — iyalāta vaḻi pōkāṭē\ntara vēṇṭiyatai tēṭu — takutiyāl acaiyāṭē\nnirantara kāraṇam veḷippaṭuttu — nērmaiyāka\ntirumpataṟku tayārāy — tīrppaḷippār aivārum.`,
    english: `Five virtues of the just judge: hearing both sides fully before forming any view; seeking what is actually true rather than what is expedient; being moved by evidence rather than by status; stating one's reasoning openly so it can be examined; and being willing to acknowledge error when shown so. These five make a judge one before whom a person can stand with hope.`,
    note: `Judicial ethics is a recurring concern in Tamil didactic literature. The fifth virtue — willingness to acknowledge error — is especially striking: it places intellectual honesty above the institutional authority of the judge's position.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு இருக்கும் இடத்தில் கடவுள் இருக்கிறார். கடவுள் இருக்கும் இடத்தில் அன்பு இருக்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Where love is, God is. Where God is, love will be. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 15,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be concealed for long",
    quintet: ["True generosity","True cowardice","True learning","True ignorance","True character"],
    tamil: `உண்மையான ஈகை — உள்ளுறும் கோழைமை\nஎண்ணிய கல்வி — ஏறாத அறியாமை\nகண்ணுக்கு கனம் குணம் — காலத்தில் வெளிப்படும்\nமண்ணில் மறைக்கலாகா — மறைவதில்லை ஐந்தும்.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaiyāṉa īkai — uḷḷuṟum kōḻaimai\neṇṇiya kalvi — ēṟāta aṟiyāmai\nkaṇṇukku kaṉam kuṇam — kālattil veḷippaṭum\nmaṇṇil maṟaikkalaṅkā — maṟaivatailla aintum.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be concealed for long: true generosity; true cowardice; genuine learning; genuine ignorance; and true character. These five cannot be permanently hidden. They rise.`,
    note: `This verse expresses a characteristic Tamil confidence in the self-revealing nature of moral reality. What matters is not the performance of virtue but its actual presence or absence, which time discloses.`,
    tamilUrai: `மழை இல்லாமல் மலர் வாடும். அன்பு இல்லாமல் மனிதன் வாடுவான்.`,
    altTranslation: `Without rain the flower wilts. Without love the person wilts. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 16,
    theme: "Five things one should learn while one can",
    quintet: ["A craft the hands can do without the eyes","A language beyond one's own","The art of listening to what is not said","How to sit with grief without running from it","The names of the plants and birds near one's home"],
    tamil: `கண் இல்லாலும் கைகள் செய்யும் கலை — தன் மொழி தாண்டும் மொழி\nசொல்லா சொல்லை கேட்கும் திறன் — துக்கத்துடன் இருக்கும் நிதானம்\nவல்லே வீட்டருகில் மரம் பறவை — பெயர்கள் கல்\nநல்ல ஐந்தும் — நாளை கற்றாற்போல் ஆகாதவை.`,
    transliteration: `Kaṇ illālum kaikaḷ ceyyum kalai — taṉ moḻi tāṇṭum moḻi\ncollā collai kēṭkum tiṟaṉ — tukkattuṭaṉ irrukkum nitāṉam\nvallē vīṭṭarukil maram paṟavai — peyarkaḷ kal\nnallā aintum — nāḷai kaṟṟāṟpōl ākātavai.`,
    english: `Five things to learn while the opportunity is present: a craft the hands can perform without the eyes; a language beyond one's mother tongue; the skill of hearing what is left unspoken; the steadiness to sit with grief without fleeing; and the names of the trees and birds near one's home. These five cannot be learned tomorrow as they can be learned today.`,
    note: `This verse has a quietly ecological dimension unusual in the Patiṉeṇ tradition. The naming of nearby plants and birds suggests that rootedness in one's immediate environment is itself a form of knowledge.`,
    tamilUrai: `சிறிய அன்பு சிறியதில்லை. தினமும் செய்யும் சிறிய அன்பு செயல்கள் வாழ்வை நிலைத்து நிற்க வைக்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Small love is not small. The small acts of love done daily keep life standing. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 17,
    theme: "Five things that make an elder worthy of respect",
    quintet: ["Living by what they teach","Giving credit to those who taught them","Remembering what it was to be young","Listening to the young without condescension","Knowing when to step aside"],
    tamil: `சொல்வதை வாழ்வில் செய் — சொல்லி தந்தார் சுட்டு\nநல்வழி இளமை நினை — நாணாது இளைஞர் கேள்\nவிலகும் வேளை அறிதல் — வீழ்க்கும் வாழவைக்கும்\nதலைவர் ஐவரும் — தாழாத முதியோர் பண்பு.`,
    transliteration: `Colvatai vāḻvil cey — colli tantār cuṭṭu\nnalvaḻi iḷamai niṉai — nāṇātu iḷaiñar kēḷ\nvilakum vēḷai aṟital — vīḻkkum vāḻavaikku\ntalaivar aivārum — tāḻāta mutiyōr paṇpu.`,
    english: `Five things that make an elder worthy of respect: living according to what they teach; acknowledging those who taught them; remembering what it felt like to be young; listening to the young without condescension; and knowing when the time has come to step aside. These five distinguish the elder who elevates those around them.`,
    note: `The fifth virtue — knowing when to step aside — is perhaps the rarest and most difficult. The Tamil literary tradition celebrates elders who recognise the appropriate moment of withdrawal without bitterness, as a final act of wisdom.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு இல்லாமல் கொடை கொடை அல்ல. அன்பு இல்லாமல் கல்வி கல்வி அல்ல.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving without love is not giving. Learning without love is not learning. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 18,
    theme: "Five things a traveller must carry within",
    quintet: ["Readiness to eat what is offered","The ability to sleep wherever night finds them","Gratitude for shelter, however small","The knowledge that all roads eventually turn homeward","An open curiosity for what is unfamiliar"],
    tamil: `கிடைத்ததை உண்ணல் — கிடைத்த இடத்தில் தூங்கல்\nபடைக்கும் புகலிடத்தில் நன்றி — பாதை திரும்பும் என்று நம்பிக்கை\nஅடைந்த புதுமையில் ஆர்வம் — அலைவோர் ஐந்தும்\nநடைக்கு உறுதுணை — நல்ல உள்ளம் தான் பயணம்.`,
    transliteration: `Kiṭaittatu uṇṇal — kiṭaitta iṭattil tūṅkal\npaṭaikkum pukalitattil naṉṟi — pātai tirumpum eṉṟu nampikkai\naṭainta putumayil ārvam — alaivōr aintum\nnaṭaikku uṟutuṇai — nallā uḷḷam tāṉ payaṇam.`,
    english: `Five things a traveller must carry within: readiness to eat what is offered; the capacity to sleep wherever night finds them; genuine gratitude for whatever shelter is given; the deep knowledge that all roads eventually find their way back; and open curiosity toward everything unfamiliar. A good heart is itself the journey.`,
    note: `Travel is a major topos in Sangam akam poetry. This verse reimagines travel ethics in the practical register of the Patiṉeṇ: the question is not the metaphysics of the journey but the dispositions required to travel well.`,
    tamilUrai: `நோயாளிக்கு மருந்தை விட அன்பான வார்த்தை சில நேரம் மேலான மருந்து.`,
    altTranslation: `For a sick person, sometimes a loving word is a better medicine than medicine. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 19,
    theme: "Five things that compound over a lifetime",
    quintet: ["The practice of a daily discipline","Small acts of honest dealing","The gentle correction of children","Unbroken gratitude toward those who helped","The habit of noticing beauty"],
    tamil: `நாளும் ஒரு கடமை — நடப்பில் நேர்மை சின்னது\nபாளும் குழந்தை திருத்தல் — பாதை காட்டியோர் நன்றி\nகாளும் கலைகண்டு ஆர்வம் — கால வழியில் ஐந்தும்\nவாளும் கூர்மை பெறும் — வாழ்க்கை செழிக்கும் நிச்சயம்.`,
    transliteration: `Nāḷum oru kaṭamai — naṭappil nērmai ciṉṉatu\npāḷum kuḻantai tiruttal — pātai kāṭṭiyōr naṉṟi\nkāḷum kalaikkaṇṭu ārvam — kāla vaḻiyil aintum\nvāḷum kūrmai peṟum — vāḻkkai ceḻikkum niccayam.`,
    english: `Five things that compound over a lifetime: daily practice of a chosen discipline; small acts of honest dealing; patient and gentle correction of children; unbroken gratitude toward those who helped along the way; and the cultivated habit of noticing beauty. Time turns these five small things into great ones.`,
    note: `The compounding metaphor is characteristic of the Patiṉeṇ tradition's interest in the long view. No single act of any of these five is impressive in isolation; their power is accumulative and visible only across decades.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்பு கற்பிக்கப்படுவதில்லை — அனுபவிக்கப்படுகிறது. கற்றுக்கொடுக்க முடியும் — காட்டுவதன் மூலம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Love is not taught — it is experienced. It can be taught — by demonstrating it. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 20,
    theme: "Five things that a poet must know",
    quintet: ["That silence sometimes says more than words","That a verse must be felt before it is understood","That the listener's world is not the poet's world","That beauty alone is not enough","That every poem is also a gift"],
    tamil: `மௌனம் சொல்வதை அறிதல் — மனம் கொண்டு கொள்வது முதல்\nவௌிலும் கேட்போர் வேறு உலகம் — வடிவம் மட்டும் போதாது\nகௌிலும் கவிதை கொடை — கவிஞர் ஐந்தும் கற்க வேண்டும்\nதௌிலும் தமிழ் உரைக்க — தகுதி ஐந்தால் வருகின்றது.`,
    transliteration: `Mauṉam colvatai aṟital — maṉam koṇṭu koḷvatu mutal\nvauḷilum kēṭpōr vēṟu ulakam — vaṭivam maṭṭum pōtātu\nkauḷilum kavitai koṭai — kaviñar aintum kaṟka vēṇṭum\ntauḷilum tamiḻ uraikkā — takuti aintāl varukiṉṟatu.`,
    english: `Five things a poet must know: that silence sometimes says more than skilled words; that a verse must first be felt, then understood; that the listener inhabits a different world and this gap must be honoured; that beauty without truth at its centre is not sufficient; and that every poem is a gift that belongs to those who receive it. These five give a poet the right to speak clearly in Tamil.`,
    note: `The final claim — that a poem belongs to its receivers — anticipates modern reader-response theory. The poet's authority derives not from possession but from relinquishment.`,
    tamilUrai: `முதல் வேர் அன்பு — இதுவே சிறுபஞ்சமூலத்தின் ஆதி வேர். அன்பு இல்லாமல் மற்ற நான்கும் வளராது.`,
    altTranslation: `The first root is love — this is the primal root of Ciṟupañcamūlam. Without love the other four will not grow. — Cutler`,
  },


  {
    num: 21,
    theme: "Five things wiser than clever words",
    quintet: ["Arriving when needed without being summoned","Doing what was asked without changing it","Leaving when the work is done","Not taking credit for another's effort","Being the same in public and in private"],
    tamil: `அழைக்காமல் வருதல் — ஆணையை மாற்றாமல் செய்தல்\nமுழுமையில் விலகல் — முயற்சி பிறர்க்கு விட்டல்\nதொழிலில் தனிமையில் ஒரே வடிவம் — துலங்கும் ஐந்தும்\nஅழகிய நடத்தை — அறிஞர் அறியும் கல்வியே.`,
    transliteration: `Aḻaikkāmal varutal — āṇaiyai māṟṟāmal ceyttal\nmuḻumaiyil vilakkal — muyaṟci piṟarkku viṭṭal\ntoḻilil taṉimaiyil orē vaṭivam — tulaṅkum aintum\naḻakiya naṭattai — aṟiñar aṟiyum kalviyē.`,
    english: `Five things wiser than any clever speech: arriving when needed without being called; doing what was asked without altering it; leaving when the work is complete without lingering for recognition; not taking credit for another's effort; and being exactly the same in public as in private. These five are finer than the finest words.`,
    note: `This verse enacts a quiet critique of eloquence. The five virtues here are behavioural rather than verbal; they suggest that the proof of virtue lies not in one's ability to speak about it but in the pattern of small, unwitnessed actions.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவு என்பது கல்வியால் மட்டும் வரும் ஒன்றல்ல. வாழ்வால் வரும், அனுபவத்தால் வரும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom does not come from learning alone. It comes from life, from experience. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 22,
    theme: "Five things not to do in anger",
    quintet: ["Make a permanent decision","Speak about another's failing","Write a letter","Walk away from someone who loves you","Swear an oath"],
    tamil: `நிலையான தீர்மானம் — நினைந்தோர் குற்றம் சொல்லல்\nகலையாத கடிதம் எழுதல் — கலங்கல் நேசர் விட்டு நடத்தல்\nசலிக்கும் ஆணை எடுத்தல் — சினத்தில் ஐந்தும் செய்யாதே\nவிலகு — விரைவில் அடங்கும் — வேண்டும் காரியம் செய் பின்.`,
    transliteration: `Nilaiyāṉa tīrmāṉam — niṉaintōr kuṟṟam collal\nkalaiyāta kaṭitam eḻutal — kalaṅkal nēcar viṭṭu naṭattal\ncalikkum āṇai eṭuttal — ciṉattil aintum ceyyāṭē\nvilaku — viraivil aṭaṅkum — vēṇṭum kāriyam cey piṉ.`,
    english: `Five things not to do in anger: make a permanent decision; speak of another's faults; write a letter whose words will outlast the emotion; walk away from someone who loves you; and take an oath. Step away from these five. The anger will pass quickly. Then do what needs doing.`,
    note: `The five acts share the quality of irreversibility — each one leaves a mark that the return to calm cannot easily undo. The verse counsels restraint not by suppressing anger but by avoiding the five actions through which passing anger becomes permanent damage.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவாளி கேட்கிறான். மடையன் பேசுகிறான். அறிவின் முதல் படி கேட்கல்.`,
    altTranslation: `The wise person listens. The fool speaks. The first step of wisdom is listening. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 23,
    theme: "Five things that grow when shared",
    quintet: ["Knowledge passed to another","Joy told to a friend","A song sung for others","Work distributed wisely","A lamp lit from a lamp"],
    tamil: `கொடுத்த கல்வி — கொண்டாடிய மகிழ்ச்சி\nபாடல் பலர்க்கு — பகிர்ந்த வேலை\nகூடல் விளக்கில் கொளுத்திய தீபம் — கூடும் வரை\nஆடல் வளரும் — அன்புடன் பகிர்ந்த ஐந்தும்.`,
    transliteration: `Koṭutta kalvi — koṇṭāṭiya makiḻcci\npāṭal palarkku — pakirnta vēlai\nkūṭal viḷakkil koḷuttiya tīpam — kūṭum varai\nāṭal vaḷarum — aṉpuṭaṉ pakirnta aintum.`,
    english: `Five things that grow rather than diminish when shared: knowledge given to another; joy recounted to a friend, which doubles in the telling; a song performed for others; work wisely distributed; and a lamp lit from another lamp, which gives without losing. These five increase with the giving.`,
    note: `The lamp-lighting image — a flame that gives without diminishing — is one of the oldest metaphors for the non-rivalrous nature of certain goods. Here it sits alongside knowledge, joy, song, and distributed labour in a broader theory of abundance.`,
    tamilUrai: `தன்னை அறிந்தவன் உலகை அறிவான். தன்னை அறியாதவன் உலகை பார்த்தாலும் அறிவதில்லை.`,
    altTranslation: `One who knows himself will know the world. One who does not know himself will not know the world even looking at it. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 24,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be hurried",
    quintet: ["The ripening of understanding","The healing of a wound, inner or outer","The building of trust between people","The growth of a child into their own character","The settling of grief"],
    tamil: `அறிவின் முதிர்ச்சி — அழுக்கற்ற காயம் ஆறல்\nநெறிவழி நம்பிக்கை வளர்ச்சி — நல்ல குழந்தை குணம்\nதுறவொத்த துக்கம் அடங்கல் — துரிதப்படுத்தலாகா\nஇறைவன் ஐந்தும் — இயற்கையின் கையில் விட்டு விட.`,
    transliteration: `Aṟiviṉ mutiṟcci — aḻukkaṟṟā kāyam āṟal\nneṟivaḻi nampikkai vaḷarcci — nallā kuḻantai kuṇam\nturāvotta tukkam aṭaṅkal — turitappaṭuttalākā\niṟaivaṉ aintum — iyaṟkaiyiṉ kaiyil viṭṭu viṭa.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be hurried: the ripening of understanding; the healing of a wound, whether of body or heart; the building of trust between people; the unfolding of a child's character; and the settling of grief. These five are in the hands of nature. Place them there and wait.`,
    note: `This verse counsels patience against the pressure to accelerate natural processes. The five things named are all specifically damaged by being rushed. The final instruction — place them in nature's hands — is among the more serene passages in the Patiṉeṇ tradition.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவு தெய்வமரக்கட்டையை போல — வெட்டட்டும், வலிமை குறையாது. பகிர்ந்தாலும் குறையாது.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom is like the wood of the divine tree — let it be cut, the strength does not diminish. Even when shared it does not diminish. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 25,
    theme: "Five things in which the small contains the great",
    quintet: ["A seed that holds the forest","A word of thanks that repays a life's debt","A child's question that undoes a scholar's certainty","A small act of kindness remembered across decades","A single moment of clear seeing that changes everything"],
    tamil: `விதையில் வனம் — வாழ்க்கை கடன் தீர்க்கும் வார்த்தை\nகுழவி கேள்வி — கோட்பாட்டை கலைக்கும் ஐயம்\nகழிந்த காலத்தில் கலந்த அன்பு — கண்ணுக்கு தெளிவான கணம்\nஅழிந்தும் ஐந்தும் — அணுவில் அகிலம் வாழ்கிறது.`,
    transliteration: `Vitaiyil vaṉam — vāḻkkai kaṭaṉ tīrkkum vārtai\nkuḻavi kēḷvi — kōṭpāṭṭai kalaikkum aiyam\nkaḻinta kālattil kalanta aṉpu — kaṇṇukku teḷivāṉa kaṇam\naḻintum aintum — aṇuvil akilam vāḻkiratu.`,
    english: `Five things in which the small contains the great: a seed that holds the forest; a word of thanks that repays a lifetime's debt; a child's question that dismantles what a scholar had thought settled; a small act of kindness remembered across decades; and a single moment of clear seeing that rearranges everything seen before and after it. In these five small things, the whole universe lives.`,
    note: `The seed-forest image is ancient in South Asian thought; its juxtaposition here with a child's question and a moment of sudden clarity grounds metaphysical insight in the ordinary domestic world — a characteristically Tamil move.`,
    tamilUrai: `கற்றவன் எங்கும் வீடு கட்டிக்கொள்வான். கல்லாதவன் எங்கும் அதிர்ஷ்டத்தை நம்புவான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The learned person builds a home everywhere. The unlearned person trusts to luck everywhere. — Ramanujan`,
  },


  {
    num: 26,
    theme: "Five things that dignify labour",
    quintet: ["Caring about the quality of what is made","Knowing why the work matters","Teaching what one knows to those who will carry it forward","Not measuring one's worth by what is paid","Taking rest without guilt when rest is earned"],
    tamil: `செய்வதில் தரம் காண் — செயலின் காரணம் அறி\nகற்றதை கடத்து — கூலியால் மதிப்பு நிர்ணயிக்காதே\nஓய்வு எடு குற்றமின்றி — உழைப்பை உயர்த்தும் ஐந்தும்\nஆய்வால் அறிவோர் — அடிமையாவதில்லை தொழிலில்.`,
    transliteration: `Ceyvatiṉ taram kāṇ — ceyaliṉ kāraṇam aṟi\nkaṟṟatai kaṭattu — kūliyāl matippu nirṇayikkāṭē\nōyvu eṭu kuṟṟamiṉṟi — uḻaippai uyarttum aintum\nāyvāl aṟivōr — aṭimaiyāvatailla toḻilil.`,
    english: `Five things that dignify labour: caring genuinely about quality; knowing why the work matters beyond its wage; passing on what one has learned; not measuring one's worth by payment; and resting without guilt when rest has been honestly earned. These five keep a worker free.`,
    note: `This verse engages with the ethics of work counter to mercantile values: worth is not determined by payment, and rest is not laziness but rightful recovery.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவின் உச்சம் தன்னை குறைத்துக்கொள்வது. அறிந்தோர் பணிவாக இருக்கிறார்கள் — அதுவே அறிவின் அடையாளம்.`,
    altTranslation: `The peak of wisdom is to diminish oneself. The truly wise are humble — that is the mark of wisdom. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 27,
    theme: "Five things that are easily broken",
    quintet: ["Trust, once doubted","A reputation, once suspected","A relationship, once spoken against","Silence, once broken carelessly","The thread of a long learning, once interrupted"],
    tamil: `ஐயப்பட்ட நம்பிக்கை — அவதூறு கலந்த பெயர்\nவைகும் உறவு — வீணாய் உடைந்த அமைதி\nகையில் கல்வியின் நூல் — கலைந்தால் திரும்பாது\nமெய்யான ஐந்தும் — மெல்லிய நூலே விழிப்புடன் வளர்.`,
    transliteration: `Aiyappaṭṭa nampikkai — avatūṟu kalanta peyar\nvaikkum uṟavu — vīṇāy uṭainta amaiti\nkaiyil kalviyiṉ nūl — kalaintāl tirampātu\nmeyyāṉa aintum — melliya nūlē viḻippuṭaṉ vaḷar.`,
    english: `Five things easily broken: trust once doubted; a reputation touched by suspicion; a relationship spoken against from within; silence broken carelessly; and the thread of a long learning, once interrupted — difficult to take up again at exactly the place left. These five are thin threads. Grow them with care.`,
    note: `The thread (nūl) is a recurrent image in Tamil ethical poetry. Here it gathers the five fragile things under a single image: each is something spun carefully over time, easily severed, and difficult to rejoin cleanly.`,
    tamilUrai: `சிறியவர்களிடம் அறிவு கற்கலாம். குழந்தைகள் தினமும் ஞானிகளாக வாழ்கிறார்கள்.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom can be learned from the small. Children live daily as sages. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 28,
    theme: "Five things that are neither given nor taken",
    quintet: ["A person's true nature","The hour of one's death","The child one is born to be","The teacher one needed and found","The language in which one first dreamed"],
    tamil: `உள்ளுறும் இயல்பு — உயிர் நீங்கும் நேரம்\nவெள்ளிட மக்கள் பிறத்தல் — வேண்டிய குரு கிடைத்தல்\nதொள்ளை கனவு கண்ட மொழி — தொகுக்க இயலாத ஐந்தும்\nகொள்ளவும் தர இயலா — கொண்டு வந்தோம் போகிறோம்.`,
    transliteration: `Uḷḷuṟum iyalpu — uyir nīṅkum nēram\nveḷḷiṭa makkaḷ piṟattal — vēṇṭiya kuru kiṭaittal\ntoḷḷai kaṉavu kaṇṭa moḻi — tokukka iyalāta aintum\nkoḷḷavum tara iyalā — koṇṭu vantōm pōkirōm.`,
    english: `Five things that can neither be given nor taken: a person's deepest nature; the hour of one's death; the particular child one was born to be; the teacher one needed and somehow found; and the language in which one first dreamed. These five cannot be transferred. We came with them. We go with them.`,
    note: `This verse turns toward the inalienable. The five items share a quality of ontological privacy: they belong to the person in a way that cannot be owned by anyone else or exchanged for anything.`,
    tamilUrai: `கேள்வி கேட்பது அறிவின் அடையாளம். கேள்வி கேட்காதவன் ஏற்கனவே எல்லாம் தெரியும் என்று நினைக்கிறான் — மடமை.`,
    altTranslation: `Asking questions is the mark of wisdom. One who does not ask questions thinks he already knows everything — that is foolishness. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 29,
    theme: "Five things that make a house a home",
    quintet: ["Food prepared and shared willingly","A place where silence is also comfortable","Elders who are listened to and not merely housed","Children who are seen and not merely managed","A threshold where the stranger is welcomed"],
    tamil: `விரும்பி பகிர்ந்த உணவு — வீட்டில் வசதியான அமைதி\nமுதியோர் கேட்கப்படல் — மழலையர் காணப்படல்\nஅடுத்தவர் உள்ளே வரவேற்பு — அணைவதற்கு ஐந்தும்\nவிடுதிக்கும் வீட்டுக்கும் — வேறுபாடு இதுவே ஐயா.`,
    transliteration: `Virumpi pakirnta uṇavu — vīṭṭil vacatiyāṉa amaiti\nmutiyōr kēṭkappaṭal — maḻalaiyar kāṇappaṭal\naṭuttavar uḷḷē varavērpu — aṇaivataṟku aintum\nviṭutikku vīṭṭukku — vēṟupāṭu ituvaē ayyā.`,
    english: `Five things that make a house a home: food prepared willingly and shared freely; a silence within the walls that is comfortable rather than tense; elders genuinely listened to; children truly seen; and a threshold where the stranger is welcomed in. These five distinguish a home from a lodging house. This is the difference, sir.`,
    note: `The direct address ("ayyā") is a Patiṉeṇ convention for especially pointed instruction. The verse draws a structural parallel between listening to elders and seeing children: both require active attention rather than mere physical provision.`,
    tamilUrai: `புத்தகம் படிப்பது அறிவின் ஒரு வழி மட்டும். மற்ற வழிகள்: மரங்களை பார்ப்பது, மழையை கேட்பது, மனிதர்களை புரிந்துகொள்வது.`,
    altTranslation: `Reading books is only one path to wisdom. Other paths: watching trees, listening to rain, understanding people. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 30,
    theme: "Five things that outlast the body",
    quintet: ["A just act done without witness","A name given to a child with care","A tree planted for shade one will never sit under","A word spoken that turned someone toward goodness","A life lived without causing needless harm"],
    tamil: `சாட்சியின்றி நீதி — சிரத்தையாய் இட்ட பெயர்\nநாட்கள் கடந்து நிழல் தரும் மரம் — நல்வழி சொன்ன சொல்\nவீட்கள் கெடுக்காது வாழ்ந்தது — இவை ஐந்தும்\nதேடுமிடம் மாறினாலும் — தேகம் மாறாது நிற்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Cāṭciyiṉṟi nīti — cirattaiyāy iṭṭā peyar\nnāṭkaḷ kaṭantu niḻal tarum maram — nalvaḻi coṉṉā col\nvīṭkaḷ keṭukkātu vāḻntatu — ivai aintum\ntēṭumiṭam māṟiṉālum — tēkam māṟātu niṟkum.`,
    english: `Five things that outlast the body: a just act done without anyone watching; a name given to a child with love and thought; a tree planted for the shade others will sit under long after the planter is gone; a word that turned another life toward goodness; and a life lived without causing needless harm. Though the place changes, the body of these acts does not.`,
    note: `The five acts here are specifically those without immediate reward or recognition. What remains is quiet, patient goodness — the inverse of spectacular achievement.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவு திமிர்ப்பை தொலைக்கும். திமிர்ப்பு அறிவை தொலைக்கும். இந்த இரண்டும் ஒரே நேரத்தில் வர இயலாது.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom removes arrogance. Arrogance removes wisdom. These two cannot coexist. — Hart`,
  },


  {
    num: 31,
    theme: "Five things a good leader keeps in sight",
    quintet: ["Those who cannot speak for themselves","The ones doing the actual work","What was promised and when","The condition of the land","The mood of those who are silent"],
    tamil: `குரல் கொடுக்காதோர் — கூலி வேலை செய்வோர்\nசொன்னது எப்போது — சொர்னமான நிலத்தின் நிலை\nமோனத்தோர் மனோநிலை — மூச்சு விடும் தலைவர்\nவானத்தை நோக்காமல் — வாழ்வில் ஐந்தும் காண்பர்.`,
    transliteration: `Kural koṭukkātōr — kūli vēlai ceyvōr\ncoṉṉatu eppōtu — corṉamāṉa nilattiṉ nilai\nmōṉattōr maṉōnilai — mūccu viṭum talaivar\nvāṉattai nōkkāmal — vāḻvil aintum kāṇpar.`,
    english: `Five things a good leader keeps always in sight: those who cannot speak for themselves; the people doing the actual work; what was promised and to whom and when; the condition of the land and natural world; and the mood of those who are silent — for silence is not always consent. These five a living leader watches.`,
    note: `The fifth item — attending to the silence of the quiet — is a counterintuitive ethical demand. In administrative ethics, the visible and vocal receive attention; this verse insists on the moral claim of those who do not press their case.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவாளி தன் அறியாமையை அறிவான். மடையன் தன் அறிவை மட்டும் அறிவான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The wise person knows his ignorance. The fool knows only his knowledge. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 32,
    theme: "Five things that spoil even good counsel",
    quintet: ["Giving advice before being asked","Speaking in public what should be said in private","Being right but unkind","Repeating advice that was already declined","Making the one advised feel small"],
    tamil: `கேட்காமல் கூறல் — கூட்டத்தில் தனி பேச்சு\nநேர்மை கொடுமையாய் — நிராகரித்தது திரும்பச் சொல்லல்\nசேர்ந்தோரை சிறியவராக்கல் — செப்பும் நற்சொல்\nதேர்ந்தும் கெடுக்கும் ஐந்தும் — தேர்ச்சி வேண்டும் ஆலோசகர்க்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Kēṭkāmal kūṟal — kūṭṭattil taṉi pēccu\nnērmai koṭumaiyāy — nirākarittatu tirumpac collal\ncērntōrai ciṟiyavarākkkal — ceppum naṟcol\ntērntu keṭukkum aintum — tērcchi vēṇṭum ālōcakarkku.`,
    english: `Five things that spoil even sound advice: offering counsel before it has been asked; saying in public what should only be spoken in private; being right in substance but delivering the truth unkindly; repeating advice that has already been heard and declined; and making the person being advised feel diminished. These five ruin even genuinely good counsel. The adviser needs skill as much as knowledge.`,
    note: `This verse makes a sophisticated distinction: being correct is not the same as being helpful. The Patiṉeṇ works frequently ask not what is true but what is required for truth to be received.`,
    tamilUrai: `பழைய அறிவை விட புதிய அறிவு மேலானது என்று சொல்லாதே. பழைய அறிவிலும் புதிய உண்மை இருக்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Do not say that new wisdom is superior to old wisdom. In old wisdom too there is new truth. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 33,
    theme: "Five things the body teaches if one listens",
    quintet: ["That rest is not a reward but a requirement","That hunger and thirst are to be honoured","That pain is information, not weakness","That breath slows the mind when the mind runs","That the body remembers what the mind forgets"],
    tamil: `ஓய்வு தண்டனை அல்ல — உண்ணுதல் தாகம் கேட்க வேண்டும்\nவாய்ந்த வலி தகவல் — வாயு மனதை மெதுவாக்கும்\nகாயம் மறந்தது தன் நினைவு — காக்கும் ஐந்தும்\nஆயம் உடல் சொல்வதை — அறிந்தோர் ஆரோக்கியமாய் வாழ்வர்.`,
    transliteration: `Ōyvu taṇṭaṉai alla — uṇṇutal tākam kēṭka vēṇṭum\nvāynta vali takavat — vāyu maṉatai metuvaikkum\nkāyam maṟantatu taṉ niṉaivu — kākkum aintum\nāyam uṭal colvatai — aṟintōr ārōkkiyamāy vāḻvar.`,
    english: `Five things the body teaches if one will listen: rest is a requirement, not a reward; hunger and thirst are honest signals to be answered; pain is information, not weakness to be suppressed; conscious breath slows the running mind; and the body carries memories the mind has released. Those who hear these five live well.`,
    note: `The medical register of the text's title reaches explicit expression here. The fifth — the body as holder of memory — touches on somatic memory, locating knowledge in the physical self rather than in the intellect.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவு இல்லாத செல்வம் திடீரென போகும். அறிவு இல்லாத ஆட்சி திடீரென விழும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Wealth without wisdom disappears suddenly. Rule without wisdom falls suddenly. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 34,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be bought back",
    quintet: ["Words said in anger that wounded","Time wasted when effort was available","Trust broken by a lie","A first impression carelessly made","A person pushed away who did not return"],
    tamil: `சினத்தில் புண்ணான சொல் — செய்யாது போன நேரம்\nபொய்யில் உடைந்த நம்பிக்கை — பொருத்தமற்ற முதல் எண்ணம்\nதள்ளி விட்டோர் திரும்பாதது — தருகிலாத ஐந்தும்\nகொள்ளவும் கொடுக்கவும் — கூடாது இனி எவரினாலும்.`,
    transliteration: `Ciṉattil puṇṇāṉa col — ceyyātu pōṉa nēram\npoyyil uṭainta nampikkai — poruttamaṟṟā mutal eṇṇam\ntaḷḷi viṭṭōr tirampātatu — tarukkilāta aintum\nkoḷḷavum koṭukkavum — kūṭātu iṉi evarinālum.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be bought back: words spoken in anger that drew blood; time wasted when effort was possible; trust broken by a deliberate lie; a first impression carelessly made; and a person pushed away who chose not to return. These five are beyond the reach of purchase, negotiation, or regret.`,
    note: `This verse focuses on irreversibility. The structure is not warning but elegy: these five are not cautioned against as future risks but recognised as things already, in some life, done and unfixable.`,
    tamilUrai: `மழை பொய்க்கலாம். அறிவு பொய்க்காது — சரியான நேரத்தில் சரியான முடிவு எடுக்க வழிகாட்டும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Rain may fail. Wisdom will not fail — it will guide one to make the right decision at the right time. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 35,
    theme: "Five things the forest knows and the city forgets",
    quintet: ["That everything takes its proper season","That waste is only matter in the wrong place","That silence is full, not empty","That nothing survives entirely alone","That the ground underfoot is itself alive"],
    tamil: `காலம் அதன் பருவம் — கழிவு தவறான இடத்தில் பொருள்\nமோனம் நிறைவு — மட்டும் ஒன்றாய் வாழாது உயிர்\nசாலும் தரை உயிரோடு — சாற்றும் வனம் ஐந்தும்\nகேளும் நகரம் மறந்தது — கேட்டால் திரும்பலாம் நாம்.`,
    transliteration: `Kālam ataṉ paruvaṉ — kaḻivu tavṟāṉa iṭattil poruḷ\nmōṉam niṟaivu — maṭṭum oṉṟāy vāḻātu uyir\ncālum tarai uyirōṭu — cāṟṟum vaṉam aintum\nkēḷum nakaram maṟantatu — kēṭṭāl tirampalām nām.`,
    english: `Five things the forest knows that the city has forgotten: everything moves in its proper season; what is called waste is only matter in the wrong place; silence is a fullness, not an emptiness; nothing lives entirely alone; and the ground underfoot is itself alive. If we listen, we can return.`,
    note: `This verse has an ecological dimension unusual in the Patiṉeṇ tradition. The contrast between forest knowledge and city forgetfulness reflects a broader South Asian literary convention, but here it is ethically charged.`,
    tamilUrai: `தன் தவறிலிருந்து கற்பது அறிவு. பிறர் தவறிலிருந்து கற்பது மேல் அறிவு.`,
    altTranslation: `Learning from one's own mistakes is wisdom. Learning from others' mistakes is superior wisdom. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 36,
    theme: "Five things a student owes a teacher",
    quintet: ["Honest account of what is not understood","The effort of genuine application","Credit given where knowledge came from","Not surpassing the teacher out of rivalry","Memory of the teacher when success comes"],
    tamil: `புரியாதது சொல் — புரிந்ததை செய்து காட்டு\nகுரு வழி அறிவை சுட்டு — கடந்து செல் போட்டியால் அல்ல\nதிரும்பி நினை வெற்றியில் — தேர்ந்த ஐந்தும் ஆசான்க்கு\nஅரும் கடமை மாணவர்க்கு — அன்புடன் கற்பவர்க்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Puriyātatu col — purintatu ceytu kāṭṭu\nkuru vaḻi aṟivai cuṭṭu — kaṭantu cel pōṭṭiyāl alla\ntirumpi niṉai veṟṟiyil — tērnta aintum ācānku\narum kaṭamai māṇavarkku — aṉpuṭaṉ kaṟpavarkku.`,
    english: `Five things a student owes a teacher: honest account of what has not been understood; the effort of genuine application; giving credit to where the knowledge came from; not surpassing the teacher through rivalry but through love of what was taught; and remembering the teacher when success arrives, not only in difficulty. These five are the rare duty of those who learn with love.`,
    note: `The fourth item is subtle: surpassing through rivalry diminishes the teaching; surpassing through love of the subject honours it. The distinction is the measure of the student's true character.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவு ஒரு விளக்கு — இருட்டை விரட்டுகிறது. ஆனால் விளக்கை எரிய வைக்க மனம் திறந்திருக்க வேண்டும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom is a lamp — it drives away darkness. But to keep the lamp burning, the mind must stay open. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 37,
    theme: "Five ways rain teaches",
    quintet: ["That patience is the posture before abundance","That arrival and withdrawal are equally natural","That small things hold great amounts","That the parched ground receives what the flooded ground cannot","That the same gift can drown or nourish depending on timing"],
    tamil: `மழைக்கு முன் பொறுமை — மழை வரலும் போகலும் இயல்பே\nசின்னதில் பெரும் சேர்க்கை — தாகத்தில் தான் ஏற்பு நிறைவு\nஅளவு நேரம் வளம் — அல்லது ஆழம் — ஐந்தும்\nமழை கற்பிக்கும் — மனித வாழ்க்கையில் பாடம்.`,
    transliteration: `Maḻaikku muṉ poṟumai — maḻai varalum pōkalum iyalpē\nciṉṉatil perum cērkkai — tākattil tāṉ ēṟpu niṟaivu\naḷavu nēram vaḷam — allatu āḻam — aintum\nmaḻai kaṟpikkum — maṉita vāḻkkkaiyil pāṭam.`,
    english: `Five lessons that rain teaches: patience is the right posture before abundance arrives; coming and going are equally natural; small vessels can hold great quantities when prepared to receive; the parched ground takes in what the flooded ground cannot — readiness determines reception; and the same gift can drown or nourish depending entirely on timing. Rain teaches these five to anyone who watches carefully.`,
    note: `Nature as teacher is a recurring motif in classical Tamil poetry. This verse's use of rain as a five-lesson teacher condenses the larger project of the akam tradition: the natural world as curriculum.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒரு கேள்வி ஆயிரம் பதில்களை பெற்றுக்கொடுக்கும். ஆயிரம் பதில்கள் ஒரே ஒரு கேள்வியை முடிக்காது.`,
    altTranslation: `One question can receive a thousand answers. A thousand answers will not finish a single question. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 38,
    theme: "Five things known only from the inside",
    quintet: ["Whether one's motive is truly what one says it is","The exact shape of one's own longing","Whether one is actually happy or merely performing it","What one regrets at three in the morning","What one would do if no one were watching"],
    tamil: `காரணம் உண்மையா — கனவின் கரை அறிதல்\nமகிழ்ச்சி மெய்யா நடிப்பா — மாலையில் மனம் வலிப்பது\nயாரும் இல்லாமல் என்ன செய்வேன் — யாருக்கும் தெரியா ஐந்தும்\nசீரான உள்ளே — சீர்மையான சாட்சி தான் தெரியும்.`,
    transliteration: `Kāraṇam uṇmaiyā — kaṉaviṉ karai aṟital\nmakiḻcci meyyā naṭippā — mālaiyil maṉam valippatu\nyārum illāmal eṉṉa ceyvēṉ — yārukku teriyā aintum\ncīrāṉa uḷḷē — cīrmaiyāṉa cāṭci tāṉ teriyum.`,
    english: `Five things known only from the inside: whether one's stated motive is one's real motive; the precise shape of one's deepest longing; whether one is genuinely happy or performing happiness; what troubles the mind at three in the morning; and what one would do if absolutely no one were watching. Only the honest witness within knows these five.`,
    note: `The most important site of truthfulness is not the public claim but the private self-knowledge. The "honest witness within" (cīrmaiyāṉa cāṭci) is a Tamil formulation of conscience that predates Western philosophical treatments of the same concept.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவாளி நாளை பற்றி சிந்திக்கிறான். இன்றை வாழ்கிறான். நேற்றை மறக்கிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The wise person thinks about tomorrow. Lives today. Forgets yesterday. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 39,
    theme: "Five things that come to those who wait with purpose",
    quintet: ["The right teacher","The right question","The moment when effort and readiness meet","The friend who arrives exactly when needed","The meaning that hides inside long confusion"],
    tamil: `தகுந்த குரு — தேவையான கேள்வி\nஉழைப்பும் தயாரும் சந்திக்கும் நேரம் — உரிய நண்பன் வருதல்\nகுழைந்த குழப்பத்தில் குறிப்பு — காத்திருந்தோர்க்கு ஐந்தும்\nஅழைக்காமல் வரும் — அர்த்தமாய் காத்தவர்க்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Takunta kuru — tēvaiyāṉa kēḷvi\nuḻaippum tayārum cantikkum nēram — uriya naṇpaṉ varutal\nkuḻainta kuḻappattil kuṟippu — kāttiruntōrkku aintum\naḻaikkāmal varum — artamāy kāttavarkku.`,
    english: `Five things that come to those who wait with purpose: the right teacher when one is genuinely ready; the right question, which arrives only after living long enough in a problem; the moment when effort and readiness finally meet; the friend who arrives at precisely the needed moment; and the meaning hidden inside long confusion. These five come without being summoned — to those who have waited with genuine purpose.`,
    note: `The distinction between purposeful waiting and mere passivity is central: the five gifts cannot be forced but come to those who continue to work and prepare in the absence of the breakthrough.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறிவை பகிர்ந்தால் அது வளர்கிறது. மறைத்தால் அது மறைகிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Wisdom grows when shared. It disappears when hidden. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 40,
    theme: "Five things said by the very old",
    quintet: ["That the years went more quickly than expected","That what seemed important mostly was not","That the body was more trustworthy than believed","That the people, not the achievements, were the meaning","That they wished they had worried less"],
    tamil: `ஆண்டுகள் விரைவாய் போயின — அவசியமென்றது அதிகமில்லை\nஉடல் நம்பகமான பங்காளி — உறவினர் தான் பொருள்\nகவலை குறைத்திருக்கலாம் — காலத்தில் கற்றோர் கூறும் ஐந்தும்\nதவறாத நினைவுரை — தலைமுறைக்கு கடத்த வேண்டியது.`,
    transliteration: `Āṇṭukaḷ viraivāy pōyiṉā — avaciyameṉṟatu atikamilla\nuṭal nampakamāṉa paṅkāḷi — uṟaviṉar tāṉ poruḷ\nkavalai kuṟaittirukkalām — kālattil kaṟṟōr kūṟum aintum\ntavṟāta niṉaivurai — talaimuṟaikku kaṭatta vēṇṭiyatu.`,
    english: `Five things said by the very old: that the years passed more quickly than anyone warned; that most of what seemed urgently important turned out not to be; that the body was a more trustworthy companion than they had given it credit for; that in the end it was the people — not the accomplishments — that gave everything its meaning; and that they wished they had spent less of their life worrying. These five must be carried forward to the next generation.`,
    note: `The testimony of the old is a form of knowledge available nowhere else. The five items are entirely predictable — and that is the point: wisdom widely known in theory is rarely practised. The verse's urgency lies in the gap between knowing and doing.`,
    tamilUrai: `இரண்டாம் வேர் அறிவு — சிறுபஞ்சமூலத்தின் ஞான வேர். அறிவு இல்லாமல் அன்பு குருட்டு அன்பாகிவிடும்.`,
    altTranslation: `The second root is wisdom — the root of knowledge in Ciṟupañcamūlam. Without wisdom, love becomes blind love. — Cutler`,
  },


  {
    num: 41,
    theme: "Five things that strengthen a community",
    quintet: ["Celebrating others' successes genuinely","Sharing resources before they are asked for","Remembering the names and histories of the place","Making space at the table for those who are new","Settling disputes before they become enmities"],
    tamil: `பிறர் வெற்றியில் மகிழ்தல் — பேசாமல் பகிர்ந்த வளம்\nஊர் வரலாறு நினைவு — உள்ளே புதியோருக்கு இடம்\nதீராது முன்னே தீர்ப்பு — திடப்பட்ட ஐந்தும் ஒரு இணைப்பு\nஓரினமாகும் ஊர் — ஒன்றாய் இருப்போர்க்கு வாழ்க்கை.`,
    transliteration: `Piṟar veṟṟiyil makiḻtal — pēcāmal pakirnta vaḷam\nūr varalāṟu niṉaivu — uḷḷē putiyōrukku iṭam\ntīrātu muṉṉē tīrppu — tiṭappaṭṭā aintum oru iṇaippu\nōriṉamākum ūr — oṉṟāy irupōrkku vāḻkkai.`,
    english: `Five things that bind a community into one body: genuinely celebrating others' successes; sharing resources before they are asked for; keeping alive the names and histories of the place; making room for those newly arrived; and settling disputes while they are still small, before they harden into enmities. For those who live as one — life.`,
    note: `The closing affirmation — the reward of communal virtue is life itself, unadorned — is characteristically Tamil: not prosperity or fame but the simple fact of living together.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் என்பது விதிகள் மட்டுமல்ல. தன்னை கட்டுப்படுத்திக்கொள்வது, பிறரை மதிப்பது, வாழ்வை ஒழுங்காக வாழ்வது.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct is not only rules. It is self-regulation, respect for others, living life with order. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 42,
    theme: "Five things that reveal a person's character",
    quintet: ["How they treat those who serve them","What they do with time that is not accounted for","Whether they keep a promise when no one would know if they broke it","How they speak about people who are not present","What they do when they make a mistake"],
    tamil: `சேவகரை எப்படி நடத்துவர் — சேர்ந்த நேரத்தில் என்ன செய்வர்\nதெரியாமல் உடைந்த உறுதி — தேவையில்லாதோர் பற்றி பேசல்\nதிருட்டான குற்றத்தில் என்ன செய்வர் — திட்டவட்ட ஐந்தும்\nகுணம் காட்டும் — குணசாலிகள் இதை அறிவர்.`,
    transliteration: `Cēvakarai eppaṭi naṭattavar — cērnta nērattil eṉṉa ceyvār\nteriyāmal uṭainta uṟuti — tēvaiyillātōr paṟṟi pēcal\ntiruṭṭāṉa kuṟṟattil eṉṉa ceyvār — tiṭṭavaṭṭa aintum\nkuṇam kāṭṭum — kuṇacālikaḷ itai aṟivar.`,
    english: `Five situations that reveal a person's true character: how they treat those who serve them when no one of status is watching; what they do with unaccounted time; whether they keep a promise when breaking it would go unnoticed; how they speak about people who are not in the room; and what they do when they make a mistake. These five are precise instruments. Those who know character know how to read them.`,
    note: `The shared quality of all five situations is the absence of external accountability — they are moments in which a person acts (or fails to act) for purely internal reasons. The verse defines character as what one is when no one is keeping score.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் உள்ளிருந்து வர வேண்டும். வெளியிலிருந்து திணிக்கப்பட்ட ஒழுக்கம் உடையும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct must come from within. Conduct imposed from outside will break. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 43,
    theme: "Five things that grow in adversity",
    quintet: ["The knowledge of who truly cares","The capacity for independent thought","Compassion for those who suffer similarly","The precise understanding of what one actually needs","Gratitude for what was there all along"],
    tamil: `உண்மையான அக்கறை — ஊணான சுயமான சிந்தனை\nதுன்பத்தில் ஈரம் — தேவையின் தெளிவு\nஇருந்ததற்கு நன்றி — இடர்க்காலத்தில் ஐந்தும்\nவருகின்றன — வலியோடு வரும் விவேகம்.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaiyāṉa akkaṟai — ūṇāṉa cuyamāṉa cintaṉai\ntuṉpattil īram — tēvaiyiṉ teḷivu\niruntataṟku naṉṟi — iṭarkkālattil aintum\nvarukinṟaṉa — valiyōṭu varum vivēkam.`,
    english: `Five things that grow in adversity and are rarely found elsewhere: clear knowledge of who truly cares; the capacity for independent thought; compassion for those who suffer similarly; precise understanding of what one actually needs rather than merely wants; and gratitude for what was present all along but unnoticed. These five come with the pain. Wisdom arrives with hardship.`,
    note: `This verse refuses the simple reading of adversity as purely destructive. The five things named are genuine goods — not consolations but actual capacities that are typically inaccessible in comfort and cultivated specifically by difficulty.`,
    tamilUrai: `சிறந்த ஒழுக்கம் என்பது யாரும் பார்க்காத போதும் சரியாக நடப்பது.`,
    altTranslation: `The best conduct is to act rightly when no one is watching. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 44,
    theme: "Five things that money cannot do",
    quintet: ["Buy the attention of someone who does not wish to give it","Replace time already spent","Make a person loved rather than merely served","Substitute for genuine knowledge","Repair what contempt has broken"],
    tamil: `மனம் வராதோர் மனம் வாங்கல் — மீண்டும் கொள்ள இயலா நேரம்\nநேசமின்றி சேவை — நிரப்ப இயலா கல்வி இடம்\nஅவமதிப்பால் உடைந்ததை — பணம் கட்டுவதில்லை\nசெய்வதில்லை பொன்னால் — சில செயல்கள் ஐந்தும்.`,
    transliteration: `Maṉam varātōr maṉam vāṅkal — mīṇṭum koḷḷa iyalā nēram\nnēcamiṉṟi cēvai — nirappā iyalā kalvi iṭam\navamatippāl uṭaintatu — paṇam kaṭṭuvatailla\nceyvatailla poṉṉāl — cila ceyalkaḷ aintum.`,
    english: `Five things that money cannot do: win the genuine attention of someone who does not wish to give it; replace time that has already passed; make a person loved rather than merely served; substitute for actual knowledge where knowledge is required; and repair the damage done by contempt. There are five things that gold cannot accomplish. These are they.`,
    note: `The Patiṉeṇ tradition frequently interrogates the limits of wealth — not arguing it is worthless but identifying five precise domains in which it is literally ineffective, governed by other kinds of value.`,
    tamilUrai: `சொன்னதை செய்வது ஒழுக்கம். செய்வதை சொல்வது நேர்மை. இரண்டும் சேர்ந்தால் சான்றோர்.`,
    altTranslation: `Doing what one says is conduct. Saying what one does is honesty. Together they make the excellent person. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 45,
    theme: "Five things that make grief bearable",
    quintet: ["The presence of those who do not try to fix it","Work that requires the body's attention","The company of those who have survived a similar loss","Permission to feel it fully rather than moving on","The passage of time, which nothing else replaces"],
    tamil: `திருத்த முயலாதோர் முன்னிலை — திணிக்கும் உடல் வேலை\nஒத்த துன்பம் வென்றோர் தோழமை — ஓடாது தாங்கும் உரிமை\nகடந்து போகும் காலம் — கவலையை தாங்கும் ஐந்தும்\nநடந்திட்ட நலிவுக்கு — நம்பிக்கை தரும் பாதை.`,
    transliteration: `Tiruttu muyalātōr muṉṉilai — tiṇikkum uṭal vēlai\nota tuṉpam veṉṟōr tōḻamai — ōṭātu tāṅkum urimai\nkaṭantu pōkum kālam — kavalaiyai tāṅkum aintum\nnaṭanttiṭṭā nalivukku — nampikkai tarum pātai.`,
    english: `Five things that make grief bearable — not by ending it but by making it possible to carry: the presence of people who do not try to fix or speed what must simply be felt; work that asks for the body's full attention; the company of those who have survived a loss like this one; the permission to feel the grief fully; and the simple passage of time, which nothing else replaces. These five are the path through the grief that has arrived.`,
    note: `This verse stands among the most practically compassionate in the collection. The five items are supports, not cures. The emphasis on permission — not being forced to move on — reflects a sophisticated understanding of the psychology of loss.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் உடையோரை சமுதாயம் மதிக்கிறது. மதிக்கப்படாத ஒழுக்கம் என்று ஒன்று இல்லை.`,
    altTranslation: `Society respects those of good conduct. There is no such thing as unrespected conduct. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 46,
    theme: "Five things that distinguish love from possession",
    quintet: ["Wanting the other's flourishing even when it moves away from you","Accepting the other as they are","Not requiring them to be grateful","Being able to let silence be comfortable between you","Remaining after the newness has passed"],
    tamil: `விலகினாலும் மலர்வை விரும்பல் — வேண்டியபடி வேண்டாமை\nநன்றி எதிர்பார்க்காமை — நடுவில் அமைதி தாங்கல்\nபுதுமை கடந்து நிற்றல் — பிணைப்பின் ஐந்தும் இதுவே\nஅதிகாரத்திலிருந்து — அன்பை பிரிக்கும் வரம்பு.`,
    transliteration: `Vilakiṉālum malarttai virumpal — vēṇṭiyapaṭi vēṇṭāmai\nnaṉṟi etirpārkkāmai — naṭuvil amaiti tāṅkal\nputumai kaṭantu niṟṟal — piṇaippiṉ aintum ituvaē\natikārattiliruntu — aṉpai pirikkum varampu.`,
    english: `Five things that distinguish love from possession: wanting the other's flourishing even when it moves away from you; accepting the other as they actually are; not requiring gratitude from them; being able to sit in silence together without discomfort; and remaining after the newness has worn away. These five are the boundary that separates love from the exercise of power.`,
    note: `This verse defines love not by feeling but by behaviour — specifically by five ways in which love differs from the need to control. The first item (wanting flourishing even when it moves away) is a demanding standard that most attachments do not meet.`,
    tamilUrai: `வார்த்தைகளில் ஒழுக்கம், செயல்களில் ஒழுக்கம், எண்ணங்களில் ஒழுக்கம் — மூன்றும் சேர்ந்தால் முழுமையான ஒழுக்கம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct in words, conduct in deeds, conduct in thoughts — all three together make complete conduct. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 47,
    theme: "Five places where the small is better than the great",
    quintet: ["A small truth over a large flattery","A small genuine gesture over a large empty one","A small household with love over a large one without","A small income honestly earned over a large one dubiously gained","A small gathering of those who know you over a crowd who do not"],
    tamil: `கொஞ்சம் உண்மை — கோலாகல பொய்யை விட\nசின்ன செய்கை — செய்யா பெரும் வாக்கை விட\nஅன்புடன் சிறு இல்லம் — அகலமான வெற்று வீட்டை விட\nநேர்மையான சிறு வருமானம் — நயவஞ்சக பெரும் ஆதாயத்தை விட\nதெரிந்தோர் சிறு கூட்டம் — தெரியாதோர் பெரும் கூட்டத்தை விட.`,
    transliteration: `Koñcam uṇmai — kōlākala poyyai viṭa\nciṉṉā ceykai — ceyyā perum vākkkai viṭa\naṉpuṭaṉ ciṟu illam — akalamāṉa veṟṟu vīṭṭai viṭa\nnērmaiyāṉa ciṟu varumāṉam — nayavañcaka perum ātāyattai viṭa\nterintōr ciṟu kūṭṭam — teriyātōr perum kūṭṭattai viṭa.`,
    english: `Five places where the small is better than the great: a small truth rather than a large eloquent flattery; a small genuine gesture rather than a grand empty promise; a small household filled with love rather than a large one that is hollow; a small income honestly earned rather than a large one dubiously obtained; and a small gathering of people who truly know you rather than a crowd who do not. In these five, less is more.`,
    note: `Each pairing identifies a genuine temptation — the impressive large thing — and a genuine good — the modest small thing — and insists on the latter's superiority. The structure of comparison is characteristic of the Patiṉeṇ works.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கமான வாழ்வு எளியது அல்ல. ஆனால் ஒழுக்கமற்ற வாழ்வு இன்னும் கடினமானது.`,
    altTranslation: `A life of good conduct is not easy. But a life of bad conduct is even harder. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 48,
    theme: "Five things that a healer must know",
    quintet: ["That the patient already knows something about their own body","That fear makes illness worse","That the will to recover is itself medicine","That kindness costs nothing and sometimes cures","That not all pain is to be removed"],
    tamil: `நோயாளிக்கு தன் உடல் தெரியும் — நோவை அஞ்சல் மிகுக்கும்\nமீளும் ஆர்வம் மருந்து — மென்மை சில நேர மருந்து\nவலி யாவும் போக்கலாம் என்று இல்லை — வைத்தியர் ஐந்தும் கற்க வேண்டும்\nகட்டுண்ட கலை — காயமும் மனமும் குணப்படுத்த.`,
    transliteration: `Nōyāḷikku taṉ uṭal teriyum — nōvai añcal mikukkum\nmīḷum ārvam maruntuaṉpu — meṉmai cila nēra maruntuvaittiyar aintum kaṟka vēṇṭum\nkaṭṭuṇṭa kalai — kāyamum maṉamum kuṇappaṭutta.`,
    english: `Five things a healer must know: that the patient carries knowledge about their own body that the healer does not have; that fear makes illness worse; that the will to recover is itself medicine and must be cultivated; that kindness sometimes accomplishes what medicine alone cannot; and that not all pain is meant to be removed — some pain is the body's necessary work. These five are the bound craft of healing both body and mind.`,
    note: `The five items together constitute a philosophy of holistic healing that treats the patient as an active participant. The fifth item — not all pain should be removed — reflects the Ayurvedic understanding of therapeutic discomfort.`,
    tamilUrai: `தன் கடமையை முடிப்பது ஒழுக்கம். தன் உரிமையை மட்டும் கோருவது ஒழுக்கமின்மை.`,
    altTranslation: `Fulfilling one's duty is conduct. Demanding only one's rights is misconduct. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 49,
    theme: "Five things that make disagreement productive",
    quintet: ["Caring more about reaching truth than winning","Being willing to state the strongest version of the other's view","Distinguishing between disagreeing with a person and disliking them","Thanking the one who showed you an error","Knowing when to stop"],
    tamil: `உண்மைக்கு முன்னுரிமை — உரத்த மாற்றுகாரர் நிலை சொல்லல்\nதனியார் மேல் தர்க்கம் — தனியார்க்கு விரோதமில்லை\nதிருத்தியோருக்கு நன்றி — தேவையில் நிறுத்தல் ஐந்தும்\nவருகின்ற குறை தீர்க்கும் — வாதம் வளமாக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaikkku muṉṉurimai — uratā māṟṟukkārar nilai collal\ntaṉiyār mēl tarkkam — taṉiyārkku virōtamilla\ntiruttiyōrukku naṉṟi — tēvaiyil niṟuttal aintum\nvarukinṟa kuṟai tīrkkum — vātam vaḷamākkum.`,
    english: `Five things that make disagreement productive: caring more about truth than being seen to win; being willing to state the strongest possible version of the opposing view; distinguishing between disagreeing with someone's argument and disliking them as a person; thanking the one who showed you an error; and knowing when the exchange has reached its natural end. These five are what turn conflict into clarity.`,
    note: `The second item — presenting the strongest version of one's opponent's view — anticipates what is now called the "principle of charity" and identifies it as a practical virtue rather than merely a logical standard.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் தன்னிலிருந்து தொடங்குகிறது — வீட்டில், தொழிலில், சமுதாயத்தில் தொடர்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct begins with oneself — it continues in the home, in the trade, in society. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 50,
    theme: "Five things said by the middle of a life",
    quintet: ["That the path taken was not the only one","That what was gained and what was surrendered were not chosen alone","That there is still time to learn what was not learned earlier","That one's children are different people from oneself","That one has been forgiven more than one has forgiven"],
    tamil: `மாற்றுப் பாதையும் இருந்தது — மனிதர்களால் வடிவு கொண்டது\nதொலைத்ததும் தொடர்ந்து படிக்கலாம் — தனி மனிதர் குழந்தைகள்\nமன்னிக்கப்பட்டேன் மன்னித்ததை விட — மத்திய வாழ்க்கை சொல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nதத்தளிக்கும் மனதுக்கு — தாங்கி வரும் உண்மை.`,
    transliteration: `Māṟṟup pātaiyum iruntatu — maṉitarkaḷāl vaṭivu koṇṭatu\ntolaitatum toṭarntu paṭikkalām — taṉi maṉitar kuḻantaikaḷ\nmaṉṉikkappaṭṭēṉ maṉṉittatatai viṭa — mattiya vāḻkkai collum aintum\ntattaḷikkum maṉatukku — tāṅki varum uṇmai.`,
    english: `Five things said by the middle of a life, if one is honest: that the path taken was not the only possible one; that what was gained and surrendered was not all of one's own choosing; that there is still time — genuinely — to learn what was not learned earlier; that one's children are entirely different people from oneself; and that one has been forgiven more than one has forgiven. These five sustain a wavering heart at the midpoint of the journey.`,
    note: `These five balance reckoning with continuing possibility. The third explicitly opens forward, and the fourth and fifth are invitations toward more generous perception of others.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் என்பது கட்டுப்பாடு அல்ல — சுதந்திரம். ஒழுக்கமான மனிதன் எந்த சூழலிலும் சுதந்திரமாக வாழ்வான்.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct is not constraint — it is freedom. A person of conduct lives freely in any circumstance. — Hart`,
  },


  {
    num: 51,
    theme: "Five things that divide those who should be united",
    quintet: ["Old grievances kept fresh","Speaking for others without their consent","Taking sides before hearing all that is true","Preferring to be right over being reconciled","Forgetting the common cause in the heat of dispute"],
    tamil: `பழைய புண்ணை புதுப்பித்தல் — பிறர் சார்பாய் பேசல்\nஎல்லா உண்மையும் கேளாமல் கோடுதல் — ஒட்டுமையை விட ஜெயம் விரும்பல்\nபொது நோக்கம் மறத்தல் — பிளவை கொடுக்கும் ஐந்தும்\nஒட்டி வாழ வேண்டியவர் — ஒன்றாய் வாழ விட மறுப்பது.`,
    transliteration: `Paḻaiya puṇṇai putuppittal — piṟar cārpāy pēcal\nellā uṇmaiyum kēḷāmal kōṭutal — oṭṭumaiyai viṭa jeyam virumpal\npotu nōkkam maṟattal — piḷavai koṭukkum aintum\noṭṭi vāḻa vēṇṭiyavar — oṉṟāy vāḻa viṭa maṟuppatu.`,
    english: `Five things that drive apart those who should stand together: keeping old wounds fresh rather than settling them; speaking on behalf of others without their consent; taking sides before all the relevant truth has been heard; preferring to be proved right over being reconciled; and forgetting the common cause in the heat of the dispute about means. These five prevent those who must live together from actually doing so.`,
    note: `The five items are each subtle forms of self-preference disguised as principle — which is what makes them particularly damaging to community life.`,
    tamilUrai: `தவறு செய்வது மனித இயல்பு. தவறை திருத்துவது ஒழுக்கம். தவறை மறைப்பது ஒழுக்கமின்மை.`,
    altTranslation: `Making mistakes is human nature. Correcting mistakes is conduct. Hiding mistakes is misconduct. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 52,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be delegated",
    quintet: ["The work of understanding one's own mind","Being present to those who need you","Making the choices that define a life's direction","Carrying one's own grief","The practice of whatever one believes in"],
    tamil: `சுயமான மனம் புரிதல் — சேர்ந்திருக்க வேண்டியோருக்கு இருத்தல்\nவாழ்க்கை திசை தீர்மானிக்கும் முடிவுகள் — வலியை சுமத்தல்\nநம்பிக்கையை செய்கையில் காட்டல் — நம்ப இயலா ஐந்தும்\nமாற்றி கொடுக்க — மாற்றாளுக்கு விட முடியா.`,
    transliteration: `Cuyamāṉa maṉam purittal — cērntirukkā vēṇṭiyōrkku iruttal\nvāḻkkai ticai tīrmāṉikkum muṭivukaḷ — valiyai cumatttal\nnampikkkaiyai ceykkaiyil kāṭṭal — nampa iyalā aintum\nmāṟṟi koṭukka — māṟṟāḷukku viṭa muṭiyā.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be delegated: the work of understanding one's own mind; being present to those who depend on your presence; making the choices that shape the direction of a life; carrying one's own grief; and the daily practice of whatever one believes. These five cannot be handed off.`,
    note: `These five are not merely things one should do alone — they are things that logically cannot be done by a proxy: their essence is precisely that they require the particular person to engage.`,
    tamilUrai: `பெரியவர்களை மதிப்பது, சிறியவர்களை பேணுவது, சம வயதினரை நட்பாக நடத்துவது — இதுவே ஒழுக்கத்தின் நடைமுறை.`,
    altTranslation: `Respecting elders, nurturing the young, treating peers as friends — this is conduct in practice. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 53,
    theme: "Five things said by those who have been truly listened to",
    quintet: ["That they felt seen for the first time in a long while","That they found their own answer in the speaking","That the listener did not try to solve what did not need solving","That the silence between words was full and not empty","That they left lighter than they arrived"],
    tamil: `நெடும் நேரத்திற்கு பின் தெரிந்தேன் — நான் பேசி நான் காண்டேன்\nதீர்க்க வேண்டாதது தீர்க்காமை — சொற்களுக்கிடையில் நிறைந்த மோனம்\nவந்ததை விட லேசாய் போனேன் — வாய்த்த கேட்போர் சொல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nகேட்பவர் கலை — கேட்கப்பட்டோர் கூறும் சான்று.`,
    transliteration: `Neṭum nērattirku piṉ terintēṉ — nāṉ pēci nāṉ kāṇṭēṉ\ntīrkka vēṇṭātatu tīrkkāmai — coṟkaḷukku iṭaiyil niṟainta mōṉam\nvantatatai viṭa lēcāy pōṉēṉ — vāynta kēṭpōr collum aintum\nkēṭpavar kalai — kēṭkappaṭṭōr kūṟum cāṉṟu.`,
    english: `Five things said by those who have been truly listened to: that they felt genuinely seen for the first time in a long while; that they found their own answer in the act of speaking rather than being told it; that the listener wisely did not try to solve what did not need solving; that the silence between the words was full rather than empty; and that they left feeling lighter than when they arrived. These five are the testimony that teaches the art of listening.`,
    note: `This verse approaches the ethics of listening from the perspective of the one listened to. The second item — finding one's own answer in the speaking — identifies listening as a condition for the listener's own thinking rather than merely a transfer of information.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொது இடத்தில் நடந்துகொள்ளும் விதம் ஒழுக்கத்தை காட்டுகிறது. மனித மதிப்பீட்டின் முதல் அளவுகோல்.`,
    altTranslation: `How one conducts oneself in public reveals conduct. It is the first measure of human worth. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 54,
    theme: "Five things about which certainty is unwise",
    quintet: ["The future","Another person's inner life","The full meaning of an ancient text","Whether one's own motives are as pure as one believes","What a child will become"],
    tamil: `எதிர்காலம் — இன்னொரு மனம்\nபழந்தமிழ் நூல் முழுப்பொருள் — பரிசுத்தமான தன் நோக்கம்\nகுழந்தை எதுவாகும் — குறித்த ஐந்தும் திட்டமாய் சொல்வது\nஅழகில் தர்ப்பண்யம் — அறியாமை கொடுக்கும் தைரியம்.`,
    transliteration: `Etiṟkālam — iṉṉoru maṉam\npaḻantamiḻ nūl muḻupporuḷ — pariculttamāṉa taṉ nōkkam\nkuḻantai etuvākum — kuṟitta aintum tiṭṭamāy colvatu\naḻakil tarppaṇyam — aṟiyāmai koṭukkum tairiyam.`,
    english: `Five things about which certainty is unwise: the future; another person's inner life; the full meaning of an ancient text; whether one's own motives are as pure as one believes; and what a child will become. Certainty about these five is not wisdom. It is the courage that ignorance lends.`,
    note: `The phrase at the close — "the courage that ignorance lends" — is a pointed reversal: the certainty in these five domains is not conviction but insufficient self-knowledge.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் பிறருக்கு தொற்றுகிறது — நல்ல ஒழுக்கம் பார்ப்பவர்களை நல்லவர்களாக்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct is contagious — good conduct will make those who observe it better persons. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 55,
    theme: "Five ways children teach their parents",
    quintet: ["By asking questions that cannot be easily answered","By noticing what adults have learned not to see","By taking seriously what adults have learned to dismiss","By reminding that play is not a lesser thing than work","By being fully present in a way most adults have forgotten"],
    tamil: `எளிதில் விடை சொல்ல இயலா கேள்வி — இல்லாமல் பார்க்கும் பார்வை\nபெரியவர் விட்டதை கவலைப்படல் — விளையாட்டை வேலை போல் கொள்ளல்\nமுழுமையான இப்போதில் இருத்தல் — மழலையர் கற்பிக்கும் ஐந்தும்\nஅழகான ஆசிரியர் — அன்றாடம் பெற்றவர்க்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Eḷitil viṭai colla iyalā kēḷvi — illāmal pārkkum pārrvai\nperiyavar viṭṭatai kavalaipaṭal — viḷaiyāṭṭai vēlai pōl koḷḷal\nmuḻumaiyāṉa ippōtil iruttal — maḻalaiyar kaṟpikkum aintum\naḻakāṉa āciriyar — aṉṟāṭam peṟṟavarkku.`,
    english: `Five ways children teach their parents: by asking questions adults have learned to stop asking; by continuing to see what adults have trained themselves not to notice; by remaining troubled by what adults have learned to accept; by treating play with the seriousness adults reserve for work; and by being entirely present in the current moment. Children are beautiful teachers. They come to parents daily.`,
    note: `This verse inverts the typical pedagogical relationship. The five things children do naturally — and lose as they age — are precisely the capacities that the Patiṉeṇ tradition identifies as virtues.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கத்தோடு வாழ்ந்தவன் மரண தருணத்தில் அஞ்சமாட்டான் — செய்வதை செய்தேன் என்று மகிழ்வான்.`,
    altTranslation: `One who lived with good conduct will not fear the moment of death — he will rejoice that he did what he was meant to do. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 56,
    theme: "Five things a trader must carry",
    quintet: ["Knowledge of the true weight of what is sold","The capacity to say no to a transaction that would harm the buyer","Memory of who kept faith in hard times","The discipline not to take the most the market will bear","The understanding that a reputation outlasts any single deal"],
    tamil: `உண்மை எடை — வாங்குவோர் கேட்டும் மறு சொல்லல்\nநம்பி வந்தவர் நினைவு — நிரம்பிய விலையை விடல்\nபெயர் ஒரு ஒப்பந்தத்தை தாண்டும் — பணியில் ஐந்தும் வேண்டும்\nகணியான வணிகன் — கால வாழ்க்கையில் வளரான்.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmai eṭai — vāṅkuvōr kēṭṭum maṟu collal\nnampi vantavar niṉaivu — nirampiṉa vilaiyai viṭal\npeyar oru oppantattai tāṇṭum — paṇiyil aintum vēṇṭum\nkaṇiyāṉa vaṇikaṉ — kāla vāḻkkkaiyil vaḷarāṉ.`,
    english: `Five things a trader must carry alongside their goods: honest knowledge of the true weight and quality of what is sold; the capacity to decline a transaction that would genuinely harm the buyer; memory of who remained faithful during hardship; the discipline not to extract the maximum the market will tolerate; and the understanding that a reputation persists long beyond any single exchange. The trader who carries these five grows across a lifetime.`,
    note: `Commercial ethics occupy a distinctive place in the Patiṉeṇ tradition. The five practices are presented not merely as moral obligations but as conditions for sustainable trade — a long-view theory of commercial virtue.`,
    tamilUrai: `உரிய நேரத்தில் உரிய இடத்தில் உரிய வார்த்தை — இதுவே ஒழுக்கத்தின் அழகு.`,
    altTranslation: `The right word in the right place at the right time — this is the beauty of conduct. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 57,
    theme: "Five things mistaken for weakness",
    quintet: ["Choosing not to retaliate","Admitting one does not know","Asking for help","Changing one's mind when shown new evidence","Weeping when grief is real"],
    tamil: `திரும்பி அடிக்காமை — தெரியாது என்று சொல்லல்\nஉதவி கேட்டல் — உண்மை கண்டு கொள்கை மாற்றல்\nநேர்மையான துக்கத்தில் அழுதல் — நேர்க்கொள்ளும் ஐந்தும்\nதீரமில்லாமை என்று — திரிபான அர்த்தம் வழங்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Tirampi aṭikkāmai — teriyātu eṉṟu collal\nutavi kēṭṭal — uṇmai kaṇṭu koḷkai māṟṟal\nnērmaiyāṉa tukkattil aḻutal — nērkoḷḷum aintum\ntīramillāmai eṉṟu — tiripāṉa artam vaḻaṅkum.`,
    english: `Five things commonly mistaken for weakness: the choice not to retaliate; admitting that one does not know; asking for help; changing one's position when shown better evidence; and weeping when grief is real. These five look like weakness to those who misread them. They are not.`,
    note: `This verse directly contests the equation between strength and hardness. The five items are all cases where apparent softness is in fact a more demanding form of honesty than the harder alternative.`,
    tamilUrai: `தன் அளவை அறிந்து வாழ்வது ஒழுக்கம். தன் அளவை மீறி வாழ்வது துன்பத்திற்கு வழி.`,
    altTranslation: `Living within one's measure is conduct. Living beyond one's measure is the path to suffering. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 58,
    theme: "Five things that make a festival meaningful",
    quintet: ["That all are included, including those on the margins","That what is celebrated has genuine significance","That the young are taught what is being commemorated","That the old are given their proper place in the ceremony","That excess does not drown out what is being honoured"],
    tamil: `ஓரமான அனைவரும் உள்ளே — உண்மையான பொருளுள்ள கொண்டாட்டம்\nஇளையோர் பொருள் கற்றல் — முதியோர்க்கு முறையான இடம்\nவளம் வெற்றுச் சோரத்தில் மூழ்காமை — வழங்கும் விழாவின் ஐந்தும்\nகளமான பங்கேற்பு — கலப்பான வாழ்க்கையின் முக்கியம்.`,
    transliteration: `Ōramāṉa anaivārum uḷḷē — uṇmaiyāṉa poruḷuḷḷā koṇṭāṭṭam\niḷaiyōr poruḷ kaṟṟal — mutiyōrkku muṟaiyāṉa iṭam\nvaḷam veṟṟuc cōrattil mūḻkāmai — vaḻaṅkum viḻāviṉ aintum\nkaḷamāṉa paṅkēṟpu — kalappāṉa vāḻkkaiyiṉ mukkiyam.`,
    english: `Five things that give a festival genuine meaning: all members of the community are included, including those most easily forgotten; what is celebrated has real significance rather than being empty habit; the young are taught what is being commemorated and why; the elders are given their proper ceremonial role; and the abundance of celebration does not drown out what is being honoured beneath the noise. These five distinguish a living festival from an empty one.`,
    note: `Festival as community ritual appears throughout classical Tamil literature as one of the primary occasions of collective identity. This verse approaches it from an ethical rather than descriptive angle.`,
    tamilUrai: `தினமும் ஒரு நல்ல செயல் செய்வது ஒழுக்கம். ஒரு தீய செயலை தவிர்ப்பது இரட்டிப்பு ஒழுக்கம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Doing one good deed daily is conduct. Avoiding one evil deed is double conduct. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 59,
    theme: "Five things that make good neighbours",
    quintet: ["Knowing when to come and when to stay away","Keeping noise and smell and light to oneself","Watching over what is left unguarded without being asked","Not carrying tales between households","Being present in trouble without requiring gratitude"],
    tamil: `வரும் நேரம் விலகும் நேரம் அறிதல் — ஒலி மணம் ஒளி சுமுகம்\nகேட்காமல் காத்தல் — கிசுகிசு கொண்டு செல்லாமை\nதுன்பில் இருத்தல் நன்றியின்றி — நல்ல அண்டை வீட்டாரின் ஐந்தும்\nசின்ன கடமைகள் — சேர்ந்து வாழ்வதின் ஆணி வேர்.`,
    transliteration: `Varum nēram vilakum nēram aṟital — oli maṇam oḷi cumukam\nkēṭkāmal kāttal — kicukicu koṇṭu cellāmai\ntuṉpil iruttal naṉṟiyiṉṟi — nallā aṇṭai vīṭṭāriṉ aintum\nciṉṉā kaṭamaikaḷ — cērntu vāḻvatiṉ āṇi vēr.`,
    english: `Five things that make good neighbours: knowing when to come and when to stay away; keeping one's own noise, smell, and light within one's own walls; watching over what is left unprotected without waiting to be asked; not carrying gossip from one household to another; and being present when trouble arrives without requiring gratitude. These five small duties are the taproot of a life lived in close proximity to others.`,
    note: `The verse turns its attention to the ethics of intimate proximity — the distinctly practical ethics of shared walls and adjoining lives. The five items are all acts of restraint and quiet service rather than dramatic virtue.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒழுக்கம் வாழ்வை எளியதாக்குகிறது. ஒழுக்கமில்லாமை வாழ்வை சிக்கலாக்குகிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Conduct makes life simple. Misconduct makes life complicated. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 60,
    theme: "Five things said by the end of study",
    quintet: ["That the more one knows, the larger the unknown becomes","That the best teachers are still learning","That the questions worth asking take a lifetime to form","That study without humility is decoration","That the end of study is the beginning of application"],
    tamil: `அறிந்தது கூட தெரியாமை கூட்டும் — ஆசான்களும் இன்னும் படிக்கிறார்\nகேட்கத் தக்க கேள்வி வாழ்நாள் எடுக்கும் — கல்வி தாழ்மையின்றி அலங்காரம்\nகற்றல் முடிவு செயல் ஆரம்பம் — கல்வியின் முடிவில் ஐந்தும் சொல்லும்\nதத்துவம் — தெரிந்தோர் தாழ்மையுடன் செல்வர்.`,
    transliteration: `Aṟintatu kūṭa teriyāmai kūṭṭum — ācāṉkaḷum iṉṉum paṭikkiṟār\nkēṭkat takka kēḷvi vāḻnāḷ eṭukkum — kalvi tāḻmaiyiṉṟi alaṅkāram\nkaṟṟal muṭivu ceyal ārampam — kalviyiṉ muṭivil aintum collum\ntattuvam — terintōr tāḻmaiyuṭaṉ celvar.`,
    english: `Five things said at the end of study: that knowing more has made the unknown larger, not smaller; that the best teachers are still learning; that the questions genuinely worth asking take a lifetime to reach; that learning without humility is merely decoration; and that the end of study is not graduation but the beginning of application. Those who have learned these five proceed with humility.`,
    note: `This verse functions as a philosophical hinge at the sixtieth position. The paradox at its core — that genuine learning increases one's sense of the unknown — distinguishes wisdom (paṇpu) from mere information (aṟivu).`,
    tamilUrai: `மூன்றாம் வேர் ஒழுக்கம் — சிறுபஞ்சமூலத்தின் நடத்தை வேர். ஒழுக்கம் இல்லாமல் அன்பும் அறிவும் வழிதவறும்.`,
    altTranslation: `The third root is conduct — the behavioural root of Ciṟupañcamūlam. Without conduct, love and wisdom will go astray. — Cutler`,
  },


  {
    num: 61,
    theme: "Five things the rain-season teaches the farmer",
    quintet: ["That the prepared ground receives, the unprepared does not","That abundance and scarcity are both temporary","That the work done before the rains determines the harvest","That no season is wasted if something is learned from it","That the earth does not remember grievances"],
    tamil: `தயாரான நிலம் ஏற்கும் — தரம் குறை மழை நேரம்\nசெழிப்பும் வறுமையும் நிலையில்லா — செய்வினை பயிர் தீர்மானிக்கும்\nவீணாகும் பருவமில்லை கற்றால் — வெறுப்பை நிலம் நினைப்பதில்லை\nஆணால் ஐந்தும் — ஆண்டவன் விவசாயிக்கு ஆசான்.`,
    transliteration: `Tayārāṉa nilam ēṟkum — taram kuṟai maḻai nēram\nceḻippum vaṟumaiyum nilaiyillā — ceyviai payir tīrmāṉikkum\nvīṇākum paruvamilla kaṟṟāl — veṟuppai nilam niṉaippatailla\nāṇāl aintum — āṇṭavaṉ vivacāyikku ācāṉ.`,
    english: `Five lessons the rain-season teaches the farmer: prepared ground receives what unprepared ground does not; both plenty and scarcity are temporary; the work done before the rains determines the harvest; no season is wasted if something genuine is learned from it; and the earth does not remember grievances — it gives equally to those who neglected it and those who tended it, in the season of return. The rain-season is the farmer's teacher in these five.`,
    note: `The fifth lesson — the earth does not remember grievances — is the most philosophically resonant: it implies that the natural world models a form of forgiveness that humans struggle to achieve.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமை என்பது பலகீனம் அல்ல. மிகப்பெரிய வலிமை — காத்திருக்கும் ஆற்றல்.`,
    altTranslation: `Patience is not weakness. It is the greatest strength — the capacity to wait. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 62,
    theme: "Five marks of a life spent with integrity",
    quintet: ["That one's word could be trusted without a witness","That those who knew one felt safe in the knowing","That one's enemies' accounts, while different, were not dishonest","That work left unfinished was finished by those who came after","That one's name, when spoken, lightened a room"],
    tamil: `சாட்சியின்றி நம்பகமான சொல் — சேர்ந்தோர்க்கு பாதுகாப்பான அறிவு\nஎதிரிகளும் நேர்மையின்றி பேசவில்லை — இடைப்பட்டவர் முடித்தனர்\nபெயர் சொன்னால் அறையில் கதவு திறந்தது — பீடிக்கும் ஐந்தும்\nஆடிய வாழ்க்கை — ஆக்கமான சான்று.`,
    transliteration: `Cāṭciyiṉṟi nampakamāṉa col — cērntōrkku pātukkāppāṉa aṟivu\netirikaḷum nērmaiyiṉṟi pēcavillai — iṭaippaṭṭavar muṭittaṉar\npeyar coṉṉāl aṟaiyil katavu tiṟantatu — pīṭikkum aintum\nāṭiya vāḻkkai — ākkamāṉa cāṉṟu.`,
    english: `Five marks of a life spent with integrity: one's word could be trusted without any witness; those who knew one well felt safe in that knowledge; even one's enemies' accounts, while naturally different, were not dishonest; work left unfinished attracted those who came later to complete it because it was worth completing; and when one's name was spoken, it lightened the room. A life that earned these five is its own testimony.`,
    note: `The verse defines integrity not through self-description but through effects on others: the five marks are all relational, describing how one was known by friends, enemies, successors, and the general atmosphere.`,
    tamilUrai: `கோபமாக இருக்கும்போது பேசாதே. பொறுமையாக காத்திரு — வார்த்தைகள் தானாக வரும்.`,
    altTranslation: `When angry, do not speak. Wait patiently — the words will come of themselves. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 63,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be inherited",
    quintet: ["Virtue","Courage","Genuine humility","The earned respect of one's peers","The capacity for wonder"],
    tamil: `நன்னெறி — நாணம் இல்லா வீரம்\nஉண்மையான தாழ்மை — ஒத்தோர் மதிப்பு\nவியப்பு கொள்ளும் திறன் — விரும்பினாலும் ஐந்தும்\nபரம்பரையால் தர இயலா — பயிற்சியால் மட்டும் வரும்.`,
    transliteration: `Naṉṉeṟi — nāṇam illā vīram\nuṇmaiyāṉa tāḻmai — ottōr matippu\nviyappu koḷḷum tiṟaṉ — virumpiṉālum aintum\nparamparaiyāl tara iyalā — payiṟciyāl maṭṭum varum.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be inherited no matter what one's birth: virtue; genuine courage; true humility; the earned respect of one's peers; and the capacity for wonder. These five come only through practice. Birth gives no advantage in them.`,
    note: `This verse is among the most democratic in the Patiṉeṇ tradition: it identifies five central human goods inaccessible to inherited advantage, insisting on a domain of virtue entirely outside the hereditary order.`,
    tamilUrai: `மரம் கனி தரும் — பொறுமையாக காத்திருந்தால். வாழ்க்கை பலன் தரும் — பொறுமையாக வாழ்ந்தால்.`,
    altTranslation: `A tree gives fruit — if you wait with patience. Life gives its reward — if you live with patience. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 64,
    theme: "Five things that make argument worth having",
    quintet: ["Both parties genuinely want to find what is true","No one is trying to win in front of an audience","The subject is one where evidence can settle the question","Both are willing to say 'I don't know' when they do not","Agreement is celebrated, not treated as defeat"],
    tamil: `இரு தரமும் உண்மை விரும்பல் — பார்வையாளர்க்கு வெற்றி வேண்டாமை\nஆதாரத்தால் தீர்க்கக்கூடிய தலைப்பு — அறியாது என்று சொல்லல்\nஒப்புக்கொள்வது தோல்வியல்ல என்று அறிதல் — ஒட்டும் ஐந்தும்\nகுறிக்கோள் நோக்கிய — கேள்விக்கான சூழல்.`,
    transliteration: `Iru taramum uṇmai virumpal — pārvaiyāḷarkku veṟṟi vēṇṭāmai\nātārattāl tīrkka kūṭiya talaipu — aṟiyātu eṉṟu collal\noppukkkoḷvatu tōlviyalla eṉṟu aṟital — oṭṭum aintum\nkuṟikkōḷ nōkkiya — kēḷvikkāṉa cūḻal.`,
    english: `Five things that make argument genuinely worth having: both parties actually want to find out what is true; neither is trying to win in front of an audience; the subject is one where evidence can in principle settle the question; both are willing to say "I do not know"; and reaching agreement is welcomed as success and not treated as one side's defeat. These five create the conditions for inquiry to be fruitful.`,
    note: `This verse addresses the conditions for productive intellectual exchange. Together with verse 49 (things that make disagreement productive), it constitutes a sustained theory of truth-seeking conversation.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமையுள்ளவன் ஒருபோதும் தோற்கமாட்டான். ஒருவேளை தாமதமாக வெற்றி பெறலாம் — ஆனால் வெற்றி பெறுவான்.`,
    altTranslation: `A patient person will never be defeated. He may win late — but he will win. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 65,
    theme: "Five things the night teaches those who cannot sleep",
    quintet: ["That the mind has its own schedule","That what was avoided during the day comes to be seen at night","That the world continues without one's management","That darkness is not the absence of light but its own thing","That by morning, something often is clearer"],
    tamil: `மனம் உடலின் நேரத்தில் இயங்காது — பகலில் தவிர்த்தது இரவில் வரும்\nஉலகம் மேய்க்காமலும் நடக்கும் — இருள் வெளிச்சமின்மை மட்டுமல்ல\nவிடியலில் தெளிவு வரும் சில நேரம் — விழிப்பின் ஐந்தும் இரவு கற்பிக்கும்\nதழைக்கும் மனதிற்கு — தவிக்கையில் பாடங்கள்.`,
    transliteration: `Maṉam uṭaliṉ nērattil iyaṅkātu — pakalil tavirtatu iravil varum\nulakam mēykkāmalum naṭakkum — iruḷ veḷiccamillāmai maṭṭumalla\nviṭiyalil teḷivu varum cila nēram — viḻippiṉ aintum iravu kaṟpikkum\ntaḻaikkum maṉatirku — tavikkaiyil pāṭaṅkaḷ.`,
    english: `Five things the night teaches those who lie awake: the mind does not follow the body's schedule; what was avoided during the day comes forward at night, since avoidance is only postponement; the world continues without one's management; darkness is not merely the absence of light but a different presence; and by morning something that seemed impossible sometimes becomes clear. The night teaches these five to those who struggle through it.`,
    note: `This verse addresses insomnia not as a medical complaint but as a form of instruction. The five lessons are characteristic of the Patiṉeṇ tradition's tendency to find ethical significance in ordinary human experiences.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமை என்பது தன்னை கட்டுப்படுத்திக்கொள்வது மட்டுமல்ல — பிறரை புரிந்துகொள்வதும் பொறுமை.`,
    altTranslation: `Patience is not only self-control — understanding others is also patience. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 66,
    theme: "Five things a speaker owes an audience",
    quintet: ["Their honest preparation","The courage to say what they actually think","The respect of not wasting the listeners' time","The acknowledgment of what they do not know","The end when the end has come"],
    tamil: `நேர்மையான தயாரிப்பு — நினைத்ததை சொல்லும் தைரியம்\nநேரத்தை மதிக்கல் — நேர்மையான அறியாமை ஒப்புக்கொள்ளல்\nமுடிவில் முடித்தல் — முன்னிருக்கும் ஐந்தும் பேசுவோர் கடமை\nநடுவில் நிற்பவர்க்கு — நன்றி இவையே.`,
    transliteration: `Nērmaiyāṉa tayārrippu — niṉaittatatai collum tairiyam\nnērattai matikkal — nērmaiyāṉa aṟiyāmai oppukkkoḷḷal\nmuṭivil muṭittal — muṉṉirukkum aintum pēcuvōr kaṭamai\nnaṭuvil niṟpavarkku — naṉṟi ivaiyē.`,
    english: `Five things a speaker owes those who come to listen: honest preparation; the courage to say what one actually thinks; the respect of not wasting the listeners' time; the acknowledgment of the limits of one's own knowledge; and ending when the end has genuinely arrived. These five are the debt owed to those who have given their attention. This is their thanks.`,
    note: `The fifth obligation — ending when the end has come — is a practical and ethical observation that the tradition recognises as surprisingly rare. Oratory is framed as service rendered to the audience, not performance for the speaker.`,
    tamilUrai: `கல்லும் தண்ணீரும் — தண்ணீர் பொறுமையாக இருந்தால் கல்லை கரைக்கும். அது தண்ணீரின் பொறுமை.`,
    altTranslation: `Stone and water — if water is patient it will erode the stone. That is water's patience. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 67,
    theme: "Five things that reveal a culture's true values",
    quintet: ["How it treats those who cannot contribute","What it counts as knowledge worthy of teaching","Who it allows to become wealthy and how","What it does with those who violate its deepest norms","What it considers too important to be said plainly"],
    tamil: `பங்களிக்க இயலாதோர் நடத்தை — படிக்க தகுந்தது என்ன\nஎப்படி செல்வம் யாருக்கு — என்ன செய்யும் மீறியோரை\nசொல்ல முடியாதது என்ன — சொல்லும் ஐந்தும் உண்மையை\nகல்லும் கலாச்சாரத்தை — காணும் கண்ணாடி.`,
    transliteration: `Paṅkaḷikka iyalātōr naṭatai — paṭikka takuntatu eṉṉā\neppaṭi celvam yārukku — eṉṉā ceyyum mīṟiyōrai\ncolla muṭiyātatu eṉṉā — collum aintum uṇmaiyai\nkallum kalāccārattai — kāṇum kaṇṇāṭi.`,
    english: `Five things that reveal a culture's actual values beneath its stated ones: how it treats those who cannot contribute anything; what it counts as knowledge worth formally teaching; who it permits to become wealthy and by what means; what it does with those who violate its deepest norms; and what it considers too important to be said plainly. These five questions, applied to any culture, show the truth of what is actually valued.`,
    note: `The fifth question — what cannot be said plainly — is the most penetrating: it identifies the zone where power makes itself invisible by becoming unspeakable.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமையற்றவன் வெற்றியை தன் கைகளால் தூக்கி எறிகிறான். பொறுமையுள்ளவன் வெற்றி தன்னிடமே வருவதற்காக காத்திருக்கிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The impatient person throws victory away with his own hands. The patient person waits for victory to come to him. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 68,
    theme: "Five things that endure in a language",
    quintet: ["The songs that mothers sang to children","The words for things that have no translation","The proverbs that encode a people's experience","The poems that made speakers understand themselves","The names given to the land itself"],
    tamil: `தாலாட்டு தாய் பாடல் — தனி மொழியாக்க இயலா சொற்கள்\nபழமொழி மக்கள் அனுபவம் — புரிய வைத்த கவிதை\nநிலத்திற்கு இட்ட பெயர்கள் — நிலைக்கும் ஐந்தும் மொழியில்\nகலையும் காலத்திலும் — கலையாது தமிழ் வாழும்.`,
    transliteration: `Tālāṭṭu tāy pāṭal — taṉi moḻiyākkā iyalā coṟkaḷ\npaḻamoḻi makkaḷ aṉupavam — puriya vaittā kavitai\nnilattiṟku iṭṭā peyarkaḷ — nilaikkum aintum moḻiyil\nkalaiyum kālattilum — kalaiyātu tamiḻ vāḻum.`,
    english: `Five things that endure in a language beyond any generation: lullabies and songs mothers sang to children; the words that cannot be translated — the ones encoding a unique perception; proverbs carrying a people's compressed experience; poems that made speakers understand something about themselves; and the names given to the land — rivers, hills, fields. In Tamil, even as times change, these five do not pass away.`,
    note: `This verse identifies what constitutes the irreducible core of a language's cultural value. The five elements are oral, memorial, and poetic — they belong to the living speech of communities rather than to the written archive.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமை ஒரு திறன் — கற்றுக்கொள்ளலாம். பொறுமையாக பொறுமையை கற்று.`,
    altTranslation: `Patience is a skill — it can be learned. Learn patience patiently. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 69,
    theme: "Five things that need repeating to each generation",
    quintet: ["That cruelty, however dressed, remains cruelty","That the weak are not weak because they deserve to be","That justice delayed is not the same as justice done","That the powerful are accountable whether they accept it or not","That each generation inherits both the good and the harm its predecessors caused"],
    tamil: `கொடுமை மறைந்தாலும் கொடுமையே — குறைவோர் அது தகுதியால் அல்ல\nதாமதமான நீதி செய்யப்படவில்லை — வலியோர் ஏற்றாலும் இல்லாவிட்டாலும்\nஅடுத்த தலைமுறை நன்மையும் தீமையும் பெறும் — ஐந்தும் திரும்பச் சொல்லவேண்டும்\nகடுமையான உண்மைகள் — காலம் கடந்தாலும் மாறா.`,
    transliteration: `Koṭumai maṟaintālum koṭumaiyē — kuṟaivōr atu takutiyāl alla\ntāmatamāṉa nīti ceyyappaṭavillai — valiyōr ēṟṟālum illāviṭṭālum\naṭutta talaimuṟai naṉmaiyum tīmaiyum peṟum — aintum tirumpacc collāvēṇṭum\nkaṭumaiyāṉa uṇmaikaḷ — kālam kaṭantālum māṟā.`,
    english: `Five things that need to be said again to each new generation: that cruelty remains cruelty however it is dressed; that those who are weak are not in that condition because they deserve to be; that justice delayed is not accomplished; that the powerful are accountable whether they accept it or not; and that each generation inherits both the achievements and the harms of those before it. These five hard truths do not change with time. They must be repeated.`,
    note: `The explicit framing — "need repeating to each generation" — acknowledges that ethical knowledge does not accumulate automatically but must be actively transmitted against the human tendency toward convenient forgetting.`,
    tamilUrai: `காத்திருப்பதும் கடினம். ஆனால் சரியான நேரத்திற்கு காத்திருப்பது மிக முக்கியம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Waiting is also difficult. But waiting for the right moment is most important. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 70,
    theme: "Five things that make a poem last",
    quintet: ["That it was true when it was written","That the image is specific and cannot be generalised away","That it leaves something unfinished for the reader to complete","That it finds the exact word that no near-word could replace","That it knows when to end"],
    tamil: `எழுதிய போது உண்மையானது — ஒரு படத்தை பொதுமையாக்க இயலாது\nவாசகர் நிறைவு செய்ய இடம் விட்டது — வேற்று சொல்லால் இடமாற்ற இயலா சொல்\nமுடியவேண்டும் போது முடிந்தது — மாறாத ஐந்தும் கவிதையை\nநீடிக்க வைக்கும் — நேர்த்தியில் சீர்மை.`,
    transliteration: `Eḻutiya pōtu uṇmaiyāṉatu — oru paṭattai potumaiyākkā iyalātu\nvācakar niṟaivu ceyya iṭam viṭṭatu — vēṟṟu collāl iṭamāṟṟā iyalā col\nmuṭiyavēṇṭum pōtu muṭintatu — māṟāta aintum kavitaiyai\nnīṭikka vaikkum — nērtttiyil cīrmai.`,
    english: `Five things that make a poem last: that it was genuinely true when written; that the central image is so specific it cannot be generalised without destroying it; that it leaves something incomplete for the reader to complete; that it has found the exact word that no near-synonym could replace; and that it knows when to end and ends there. These five are the precision that gives a poem its duration.`,
    note: `The five qualities of lasting poetry are all forms of restraint: truth rather than effect, specificity rather than generality, incompleteness rather than explanation, precision rather than approximation, brevity rather than extension.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமையாக வாழ்பவன் நீண்ட ஆயுள் வாழ்வான். பொறுமையற்றவன் ஆயுளையே சுருக்கிக்கொள்கிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `One who lives with patience will live long. The impatient person shortens his own life. — Hart`,
  },


  {
    num: 71,
    theme: "Five things that make old age honourable",
    quintet: ["Having fewer regrets than one feared","Still being curious about what one does not know","Being able to say 'I was wrong about that'","Finding younger people interesting, not threatening","Not needing the last word"],
    tamil: `அஞ்சியதை விட குறைவான வருத்தங்கள் — அறியாதது இன்னும் ஆர்வம்\nதவறென்று சொல்லும் திறன் — இளையோர் அச்சுறுத்துவதில்லை\nகடைசி சொல் வேண்டாமை — கவிழ்க்கும் ஐந்தும்\nமுதுமையை — முத்துமணி ஆக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Añciyatai viṭa kuṟaivāṉa varutaṅkaḷ — aṟiyātatu iṉṉum ārvam\ntavaṟeṉṟu collum tiṟaṉ — iḷaiyōr accuṟuttuvatailla\nkaṭaici col vēṇṭāmai — kaviḻkkum aintum\nmutumaiayai — muttamaṇi ākkum.`,
    english: `Five things that make old age honourable rather than merely old: having fewer regrets than one feared; still being curious about what one does not yet know; being able to say "I was wrong" freely and without embarrassment; finding the young interesting rather than threatening; and not needing the last word. These five make old age not merely a duration but a pearl.`,
    note: `The five qualities are all forms of continued openness — to error, to others, to new knowledge, to yielding. They are the marks of an age that has loosened its grip rather than tightened it.`,
    tamilUrai: `குழந்தைக்கு பொறுமை கற்பி. அது கல்வியை விட மேலான கொடை.`,
    altTranslation: `Teach a child patience. That is a greater gift than education. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 72,
    theme: "Five things harder to find than wealth",
    quintet: ["Someone who listens without planning their reply","A person whose praise means something because they also criticise","One who tells you what you need to hear","A friend who is still there after you have made a serious mistake","Someone who celebrates your success as if it were their own"],
    tamil: `பதிலை திட்டமிடாது கேட்பவர் — புகழ்வதும் கண்டிப்பதும் இருப்பவர்\nவேண்டியதை சொல்பவர் — தீவிர தவறுக்கு பின்னும் நண்பர்\nவெற்றியில் சொந்தமாய் மகிழ்வர் — வேரான ஐந்தும் செல்வத்தை விட அரியது\nசேர்ந்து தேடு — செய்வதன் பலன் இவர்கள்.`,
    transliteration: `Patilai tiṭṭamiṭātu kēṭpavar — pukaḻvatunm kaṇṭippatunm irupavar\nvēṇṭiyatai colpavar — tīvira tavaṟukku piṉṉum naṇpar\nveṟṟiyil contamāy makiḻvar — vērāṉa aintum celvatai viṭa ariyatu\ncērntu tēṭu — ceyvataṉ paṉ ivārkaḷ.`,
    english: `Five things harder to find than wealth: a person who listens without already forming their reply; someone whose praise means something because they are equally willing to criticise honestly; one who tells you what you need rather than what you have implied you wish to hear; a friend who remains present after a serious mistake; and someone who celebrates your success with a joy that feels genuinely like their own. Seek these five. They are rarer than riches, and worth more.`,
    note: `The five people described are characterised by a quality of presence that money cannot purchase — each is valuable precisely because they are not responsive to those kinds of inducements.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒரு நல்ல முடிவு எடுக்க நேரம் ஆகும். அவசரமான முடிவு அவமானத்திற்கு வழிவகுக்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Making a good decision takes time. A hasty decision leads to shame. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 73,
    theme: "Five things that a river knows",
    quintet: ["That the direction is always forward","That obstacles are to be gone around, not through","That depth is not always visible from the surface","That the sea it is moving toward does not come to it","That it carries what is given to it whether it wants to or not"],
    tamil: `திசை எப்போதும் முன்னோக்கி — தடைகள் சுற்றி செல்ல வேண்டும்\nஆழம் மேலே தெரிவதில்லை — ஆழி அதனை நோக்கி வராது\nகொடுத்ததை சுமக்கும் விரும்பினாலும் இல்லாவிட்டாலும் — ஆறு அறியும் ஐந்தும்\nநீரோட்டம் — நீதியான வாழ்க்கை கற்பிக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Ticai eppōtum muṉṉōkki — taṭaikaḷ cuṟṟi cella vēṇṭum\nāḻam mēlē terivatailla — āḻi ataṉai nōkki varātu\nkoṭuttātai cumakkum virumpiṉālum illāviṭṭālum — āṟu aṟiyum aintum\nnīrōṭṭam — nītiyāṉa vāḻkkai kaṟpikkum.`,
    english: `Five things that a river knows: the direction is always forward with no returning; obstacles are to be navigated around rather than broken through; depth cannot be read from the surface appearance; the sea does not come to meet it — the river must make its own way; and it carries what is deposited in it whether or not it wishes to. A river's flow teaches a just life.`,
    note: `The river as ethical wisdom appears throughout Tamil literature. This verse articulates a philosophy of purposeful, accommodating, and self-aware movement — forward, not forced, aware of depth, responsible for what is carried.`,
    tamilUrai: `அறுவடை காலத்திற்காக விவசாயி காத்திருக்கிறான். அதே பொறுமை வாழ்க்கையிலும் வேண்டும்.`,
    altTranslation: `The farmer waits for the time of harvest. The same patience is needed in life. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 74,
    theme: "Five things that make a question worth asking",
    quintet: ["That one genuinely does not know the answer","That the answer would actually change something","That it is asked from curiosity rather than challenge","That it is specific enough to be answerable","That one is prepared to sit with the answer even if uncomfortable"],
    tamil: `விடை தெரியாமை — விடை ஏதோவொன்றை மாற்றும்\nஆர்வத்தால் கேட்டல் — ஆய்வு கூடிய குறிப்பிட்ட கேள்வி\nஅசைக்கும் விடைக்கும் தயாரிருத்தல் — அமையும் ஐந்தும்\nகேட்டால் மட்டும் — கேள்வி மதிப்புடையது.`,
    transliteration: `Viṭai teriyāmai — viṭai ētōvontai māṟṟum\nārvattāl kēṭṭal — āyvu kūṭiya kuṟippiṭṭā kēḷvi\nacaikkum viṭaikkum tayārirruttal — amaiyum aintum\nkēṭṭāl maṭṭum — kēḷvi matippuṭaiyatu.`,
    english: `Five things that make a question worth asking: genuine ignorance of the answer; the answer would actually change something if found; it is asked from curiosity rather than as a challenge; it is specific enough to be genuinely answerable; and one is prepared to sit with the answer even if it disturbs what one previously believed. A question with these five qualities is worth the asking.`,
    note: `This verse pairs with verses 49 and 64 to form a trilogy on the ethics of intellectual inquiry. Here the focus narrows to the individual act of asking, identifying it as a form of ethical action that can be performed more or less well.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமையுள்ளவன் சூழ்நிலையை புரிந்துகொள்கிறான். பொறுமையற்றவன் சூழ்நிலையிலேயே சிக்கிக்கொள்கிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The patient person understands the situation. The impatient person gets trapped in the situation. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 75,
    theme: "Five things the body does for the mind without being asked",
    quintet: ["Returns to balance after extremes","Signals before breaking down","Stores pleasure alongside pain","Generates rhythm when given silence","Heals, given time and attention"],
    tamil: `கடைசிகளுக்கு பின் சமநிலை — சேதமாவதற்கு முன் சமிக்கை\nவலியுடன் இன்பம் சேர்த்து வைத்தல் — மௌனத்தில் தாளம் கொடுத்தல்\nநேரமும் கவனமும் இருந்தால் குணமடைதல் — நிதமான ஐந்தும்\nமனம் கேளாமல் — மாமருத்துவன் உடல் செய்யும்.`,
    transliteration: `Kaṭaicikaḷukku piṉ camanilai — cētamāvataṟku muṉ camikkai\nvaliyuṭaṉ iṉpam cērntu vaitttal — mauṉattil tāḷam koṭutttal\nnēramum kavanamuṉm iruntāl kuṇamaṭaittal — nitamāṉa aintum\nmaṉam kēḷāmal — māmaruttuvan uṭal ceyyum.`,
    english: `Five things the body does for the mind without being asked: returns to balance after extremes; signals its own distress before actual breakdown; stores pleasure in the same tissues that hold pain; generates internal rhythm when given silence; and heals, given adequate time and genuine attention. The body is the great physician. It works for the mind daily, without request.`,
    note: `This verse continues the strand of embodied wisdom from verse 33. Together they constitute a philosophy of somatic intelligence: the body is not merely an instrument of the mind but a form of knowledge in itself.`,
    tamilUrai: `கோபம் ஒரு நிமிட உணர்வு. பொறுமை ஒரு வாழ்நாள் திறன். ஒரு நிமிட கோபம் வாழ்நாள் நட்பை அழிக்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Anger is a one-minute feeling. Patience is a lifetime skill. A one-minute anger can destroy a lifetime friendship. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 76,
    theme: "Five things that last longer than stone",
    quintet: ["A true word spoken at the right moment","A decision made for the right reason","A kindness given without expectation","A name spoken in gratitude by those helped","Work done well when no one was watching"],
    tamil: `சரியான நேரத்தில் உண்மையான சொல் — சரியான காரணத்தில் முடிவு\nஎதிர்பார்ப்பில்லாது கருணை — உதவியோரின் கணக்கில் பெயர்\nபார்ப்பதில்லை என்று நன்றாய் செய்த வேலை — பாக்கியமான ஐந்தும்\nகற்களை விட — கால வழியில் நிலைக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Cariyāṉa nērattil uṇmaiyāṉa col — cariyāṉa kāraṇattil muṭivu\netirpārppillātu karuṇai — utaviyōriṉ kaṇakkil peyar\npārppatailla eṉṟu naṉṟāy ceyta vēlai — pākkiyamāṉa aintum\nkaṟkaḷai viṭa — kāla vaḻiyil nilaikkum.`,
    english: `Five things that last longer than stone: a true word spoken at the right moment; a decision made for the genuinely right reason; a kindness given without expectation of any return; a name spoken with gratitude by those helped; and work done well when no one was watching. These five outlast stone.`,
    note: `The verse inverts the expectation: the five things named are immaterial and small-scale, yet more durable than the most imposing physical structure. Permanence lies in acts of integrity rather than in physical scale.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமையுள்ளவன் கற்றுக்கொள்கிறான். பொறுமையற்றவன் திரும்ப திரும்ப தவறு செய்கிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `The patient person learns. The impatient person makes the same mistake again and again. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 77,
    theme: "Five things every craft teaches beyond itself",
    quintet: ["That excellence requires repetition willingly sustained","That the material has its own nature and must be understood","That the best work looks easier than it is","That a mistake is not a failure but a correction waiting","That mastery is the moment one stops thinking about technique"],
    tamil: `சிறப்பு விரும்பி மீண்டும் மீண்டும் செய்தல் — பொருள் இயல்பை அறிதல்\nசிறந்த வேலை எளிதாய் தோன்றும் — தவறு திருத்தமாகும்\nஉத்தி பற்றி யோசிக்காமல் செய்யும் நேரம் தேர்ச்சி — கலை ஐந்தும் கற்பிக்கும்\nகைவினைக்கு அப்பால் — கடமையை.`,
    transliteration: `Ciṟappu virumpi mīṇṭum mīṇṭum ceyttal — poruḷ iyalpai aṟital\nciṟanta vēlai eḷitāy tōṉṟum — tavaṟu tiruttamākum\nutti paṟṟi yōcikkāmal ceyyum nēram tērcci — kalai aintum kaṟpikkum\nkaiviniakku appāl — kaṭamaiyai.`,
    english: `Five things every craft teaches beyond its own domain: excellence requires repetition sustained by genuine willingness; the material has its own nature that must be respected, not imposed upon; the best work looks far easier than it was; a mistake is not a failure but the material showing where a correction is needed; and mastery is the state in which one has stopped thinking about technique and the work simply happens. Every craft teaches these five lessons about duty itself.`,
    note: `This verse applies the Patiṉeṇ tradition's interest in practical ethics to craft knowledge. The fifth — mastery as the end of conscious technique — echoes the concept of effortless action, naturalised into the Tamil ethical context.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஆழமான ஆற்று ஓட்டம் அமைதியாக இருக்கும். ஆழமான மனிதனும் அப்படி.`,
    altTranslation: `The flow of a deep river is quiet. A person of depth is the same. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 78,
    theme: "Five things that a leader must accept",
    quintet: ["Being blamed for what one did not do","That not everyone will understand the decision in time","That the decision made today may look wrong tomorrow","That those led do not owe gratitude","That the last to know are often the ones the decision affects most"],
    tamil: `செய்யாததற்கு குற்றம் — சிலர் காலத்தில் மட்டும் புரிவர்\nஇன்றைய முடிவு நாளை தவறாகலாம் — நடத்தப்படுவோர் நன்றி சொல்ல வேண்டியதில்லை\nகடைசியில் தெரிவோர் பெரும்பாலும் அதிக பாதிப்பு — தலைவர் ஏற்க வேண்டும் ஐந்தும்\nகட்டியங்கூறா — கல்வி பெற்ற ஆட்சியர்க்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Ceyyātataṟku kuṟṟam — cilar kālattil maṭṭum puriyār\niṉṟaiya muṭivu nāḷai tavaṟākalam — naṭattappaṭuvōr naṉṟi colla vēṇṭiyatailla\nkaṭaiciyil terivōr perumpalum atika pātippu — talaivar ēṟka vēṇṭum aintum\nkaṭṭiyaṅkūṟā — kalvi peṟṟā āṭciyarkku.`,
    english: `Five things a leader must accept as part of the role: being blamed for consequences one did not cause; that some will understand the decision only later; that today's decision may look wrong tomorrow; that those who are led do not owe gratitude — service is given for their sake; and that those who learn last about a decision are often those it affects most. A leader educated in governance accepts these five.`,
    note: `This verse explicitly rejects the expectation of gratitude in leadership. The five items constitute a philosophy of service rather than power: the leader who demands thanks has confused the direction of the obligation.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொறுமை என்பது தன்னை மறந்து பிறரை பார்ப்பது. தன்னை மறந்தவன் எல்லாவற்றையும் பெறுவான்.`,
    altTranslation: `Patience is to forget oneself and see others. One who forgets himself gains everything. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 79,
    theme: "Five things about which arguments are rarely worth having",
    quintet: ["What cannot be changed","Another person's taste","Whether the past should have been different","The value of what one loves","Who suffered more"],
    tamil: `மாற்ற இயலாதது — மற்றவர் விருப்பம்\nகடந்தது வேறாக இருந்திருக்கலாமா — காதலிப்பதன் மதிப்பு\nயாருக்கு அதிக துன்பம் — வாதத்திற்கு ஐந்தும்\nபெரும்பாலும் தேவையில்லை — பேசும் வழியில் விட்டிடு.`,
    transliteration: `Māṟṟa iyalātatu — maṟṟavar viruppam\nkaṭantatu vēṟāka iruntirukkalāmā — kātalippataṉ matippu\nyārukku atika tuṉpam — vātattirku aintum\nperumpālum tēvaiyilla — pēcum vaḻiyil viṭṭiṭu.`,
    english: `Five things about which argument is rarely worth the effort: what is already done and cannot be changed; another person's taste; whether the past should have been otherwise; the value of what someone loves; and who suffered more. About these five, argument is mostly unnecessary. Let them pass in conversation.`,
    note: `The list is notable for its inclusion of competing claims to suffering — which the verse identifies as a particular trap since it converts shared pain into rivalry.`,
    tamilUrai: `நம்பிக்கையும் பொறுமையும் சேர்ந்தால் எந்த கஷ்டமும் கடக்கலாம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Faith and patience together — any hardship can be crossed. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 80,
    theme: "Five things learned from sitting still",
    quintet: ["That the mind is rarely where the body is","That most urgency is invented","That what one was avoiding has waited patiently","That silence has texture","That the present moment is usually sufficient"],
    tamil: `மனம் உடல் இருக்கும் இடத்தில் அரிதாய் இருக்கும் — அவசரம் பெரும்பாலும் கட்டமைக்கப்பட்டது\nதவிர்க்கப்பட்டது பொறுமையாய் காத்திருந்தது — அமைதிக்கு அமைப்பு உண்டு\nஇந்த நேரம் பெரும்பாலும் போதுமானது — நிலையான ஐந்தும்\nஇருக்கும் இடத்தில் — இருந்தால் கிடைக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Maṉam uṭal irukkum iṭattil aritāy irukkum — avacaram perumpālum kaṭṭamaikkapp paṭṭatu\ntavirkkappaṭṭatu poṟumaiyāy kāttiruntatu — amaiti kkku amaippu uṇṭu\ninta nēram perumpālum pōtumāṉatu — nilaiyāṉa aintum\nirukkum iṭattil — iruntāl kiṭaikkum.`,
    english: `Five things learned by sitting still: the mind is rarely in the place where the body sits; most of what feels urgent has been constructed as urgent and would survive being delayed; what one has been avoiding has been waiting patiently; silence is textured and full; and the present moment is usually sufficient for what it requires. These five come only from being still.`,
    note: `The five lessons are psychological rather than metaphysical: they address the conditions under which a person can perceive clearly rather than through the distortions of urgency, avoidance, and distraction.`,
    tamilUrai: `நான்காம் வேர் பொறுமை — சிறுபஞ்சமூலத்தின் நிலைமை வேர். பொறுமை இல்லாமல் அன்பும் அறிவும் ஒழுக்கமும் சோர்ந்துவிடும்.`,
    altTranslation: `The fourth root is patience — the root of steadiness in Ciṟupañcamūlam. Without patience, love, wisdom, and conduct will tire. — Cutler`,
  },


  {
    num: 81,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be rushed in a child",
    quintet: ["The settling of temperament","The readiness to learn to read","The capacity for sustained friendship","The understanding of consequence","The desire to be responsible"],
    tamil: `குணம் அமைதல் — கல்வி கற்க தயாரிருத்தல்\nநீடித்த நட்பு கொள்ளல் — நேரடி விளைவு புரிதல்\nபொறுப்பை விரும்பல் — விரைக்க இயலா ஐந்தும்\nகுழந்தைகளில் — குருவால் அல்ல காலத்தால் வரும்.`,
    transliteration: `Kuṇam amaittal — kalvi kaṟka tayārirruttal\nnīṭitta naṭpu koḷḷal — nēraṭi viḷaivu purittal\npoṟuppai virumpal — viraikka iyalā aintum\nkuḻantaikaḷil — kuruvāl alla kālattāl varum.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be rushed in a child without causing damage: the settling of a temperament; the readiness to learn to read; the capacity for sustained friendship; the genuine understanding of consequence; and the desire to take on responsibility. These five come from time, not from the teacher's schedule.`,
    note: `The Patiṉeṇ tradition trusts the natural unfolding of human capacities over forced acceleration. The five things are not merely slow to develop but are specifically harmed by being rushed.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடை என்பது பொருளை கொடுப்பது மட்டுமல்ல. நேரம், அன்பு, கவனிப்பு கொடுப்பதும் கொடை.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving is not only giving material things. Giving time, love, attention is also giving. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 82,
    theme: "Five things that make a story worth telling",
    quintet: ["That something actually happened, even if changed in the telling","That it surprised the teller in the living of it","That it asks something of the listener","That it leaves room for the listener's own experience","That it knows when it is over"],
    tamil: `நடந்தது உண்டு சொல்லல் மாறினாலும் — சொல்வோருக்கு வாழ்வில் ஆச்சரியம்\nகேட்போரை கேட்கும் கதை — கேட்போர் அனுபவத்திற்கு இடம்\nமுடிந்தபோது முடியும் — மாட்சிமையான ஐந்தும்\nகதையை — காலம் கடந்தும் வாழ வைக்கும்.`,
    transliteration: `Naṭantatu uṇṭu collal māṟiṉālum — colvōrkku vāḻvil āccaryam\nkēṭpōrai kēṭkum katai — kēṭpōr aṉupavattirkku iṭam\nmuṭintapōtu muṭiyum — māṭcimaiyāṉa aintum\nkataiyai — kālam kaṭantum vāḻa vaikkum.`,
    english: `Five things that make a story worth telling: something actually happened at its core, even if the telling has shaped it; the story surprised the person who lived it; it makes a demand on the listener rather than just offering information; it leaves a space the listener can enter with their own experience; and it ends when it is over and does not continue past the moment of completion. These five give a story its life across time.`,
    note: `The five qualities identify the story as a shared act — each quality describes a form of relationship between teller and listener, oriented toward the listener's active participation.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடுக்கும்போது கொடுப்பவன் செழிக்கிறான். வாங்கும்போது வாங்குபவன் கடன்படுகிறான்.`,
    altTranslation: `When one gives, the giver flourishes. When one receives, the receiver incurs a debt. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 83,
    theme: "Five things the stars teach on a clear night",
    quintet: ["That one's own concerns are smaller than they feel","That there are forms of order too large to manage","That light travels, and what one sees is already past","That darkness is not emptiness but distance","That one small light in darkness is still a light"],
    tamil: `உணர்வதை விட சிறிய கவலைகள் — நிர்வகிக்க இயலா வரிசை\nகாண்பது ஏற்கனவே கடந்தது — இருள் வெறுமையல்ல தூரம்\nஇருட்டில் ஒரு சின்ன விளக்கு விளக்கே — விண்மீன்கள் கற்பிக்கும் ஐந்தும்\nதெளிந்த இரவில் — தெரிந்தோர் கேட்டுக்கொள்வர்.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇarvatai viṭa ciṟiya kavalakaḷ — nirvakikka iyalā varicai\nkāṇpatu ēṟkaṉavē kaṭantatu — iruḷ veṟumaiyalla tūram\niruṭṭil oru ciṉṉa viḷakku viḷakkē — viṇmīṉkaḷ kaṟpikkum aintum\nteḷinta iravil — terintōr kēṭṭukkoḷvar.`,
    english: `Five things the stars teach on a clear night: one's immediate concerns are smaller than they feel; there are forms of order too vast to manage; the light arriving now left its source long ago — what one sees is already the past; darkness is not emptiness but simply distance, and therefore not fearful; and one small light in darkness is still, undeniably, a light. Those who know how to listen hear these five.`,
    note: `The fifth lesson — small light in darkness is still light — is the most immediately applicable, connecting the vastness of the other four to the most intimate human scale.`,
    tamilUrai: `கையை திறந்து கொடு — கூடவே மனத்தையும் திறந்து கொடு. மனம் திறவாமல் கொடுத்தால் அது கொடை அல்ல.`,
    altTranslation: `Give with an open hand — and with it open the heart. If given without an open heart, it is not a gift. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 84,
    theme: "Five things every generation must discover for itself",
    quintet: ["That youth does not last","That love is more complicated than it appeared","That the work one thought would be finished keeps going","That the people one dismissed were often right","That the generation before them was also young once, and also afraid"],
    tamil: `இளமை நிலையில்லை — அன்பு தோன்றியதை விட சிக்கலானது\nமுடிந்தது என்ற வேலை தொடரும் — மதியாதோர் பெரும்பாலும் சரியே\nமுந்திய தலைமுறையும் இளமையாய் அஞ்சியிருந்தது — மீண்டும் ஐந்தும்\nஒவ்வொரு தலைமுறையும் — ஒற்றை குரலால் கண்டறியும்.`,
    transliteration: `Iḷamai nilaiyilla — aṉpu tōṉṟiyatai viṭa cikkalanātu\nmuṭintatu eṉṟa vēlai toṭarum — matiyātōr perumpālum cariyē\nmuntiya talaimuṟaiyum iḷamaiyāy añciyiruntatu — mīṇṭum aintum\novvoṉṟu talaimuṟaiyum — oṟṟai kurāl kāṇṭaṟiyum.`,
    english: `Five things every generation must discover for itself, because being told does not suffice: that youth does not last; that love is more complex than imagined; that the work one thought would be finished continues beyond every apparent ending; that those one dismissed in the confidence of youth were often more right than they seemed; and that the generation before had also been young once, also uncertain, also afraid. These five must be found again by each new generation, in its own voice.`,
    note: `This verse explicitly acknowledges the limits of transmission. Some knowledge cannot be passed directly — it must be earned. The five things are structural features of human experience that language can point toward but only living can actually impart.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடை கடலை போல் — கொடுத்தாலும் குறையாது. தருவதில் வளர்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving is like the ocean — even when given, it does not diminish. It grows in the giving. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 85,
    theme: "Five things that belong to everyone equally",
    quintet: ["The light of the morning","The sound of rain","The movement of wind","The dark that comes equally to all at night","The capacity for kindness"],
    tamil: `காலை வெளிச்சம் — கடலளவு மழை சப்தம்\nகாற்றின் தொடுகை — இரவு அனைவருக்கும் சம இருள்\nகருணை கொள்ளும் திறன் — கடவுள் கொடுத்த ஐந்தும்\nஅனைவர்க்கும் சமம் — ஐம்பொறி உணரும் பொது.`,
    transliteration: `Kālai veḷiccam — kaṭalaḷavu maḻai cappam\nkāṟṟiṉ toṭukai — iravu anaivārukku cama iruḷ\nkaruṇai koḷḷum tiṟaṉ — kaṭavuḷ koṭutta aintum\nanaivarkku camam — aimpori uṇarum potu.`,
    english: `Five things that belong to everyone equally: the light of the morning; the sound of rain; the movement of wind against the skin; the darkness that comes equally to every person at night; and the capacity for kindness, present in every human being regardless of what they have been given or denied. These five were given to all. They are the common estate.`,
    note: `By identifying five things already equally distributed, the verse implies that the hierarchies governing other goods are not natural but constructed. The fifth item — the capacity for kindness — insists that moral capacity is a universal endowment, not a privilege of birth.`,
    tamilUrai: `தேவையானவனுக்கு கொடு — தேவையில்லாதவனுக்கு கொடுப்பது வீண்.`,
    altTranslation: `Give to one who needs — giving to one who does not need is waste. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 86,
    theme: "Five things that silence says",
    quintet: ["That the speaker trusts the listener to fill it","That what was said has been received and is being held","That some things are too large for words to carry unaided","That the relationship is old enough not to need constant sound","That something important is approaching and will declare itself"],
    tamil: `கேட்போர் நம்பிக்கையில் இடம் விடல் — சொல்லப்பட்டது ஏந்திக்கொண்டிருக்கிறோம்\nசொல்லால் மட்டும் சுமக்க இயலாதது — சத்தமின்றி நடக்கும் உறவு\nமுக்கியமான ஏதோவொன்று வருகிறது — மவுனம் சொல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nவார்த்தைகளில்லா — வாழ்க்கையின் பேச்சு.`,
    transliteration: `Kēṭpōr nampikkkaiyil iṭam viṭal — collappaṭṭatu ēntikoṇṭirukkiṟōm\ncollāl maṭṭum cumakka iyalātatu — cattamiṉṟi naṭakkum uṟavu\nmukkiyamāṉa ētōvonttu varukiṟatu — mavuṉam collum aintum\nvārtaikaḷillā — vāḻkkaiyiṉ pēccu.`,
    english: `Five things that silence says: that the speaker trusts the listener enough to leave a space; that what has just been said is being held and honoured; that what is present is too large for words alone and needs quiet alongside them; that the relationship is deep enough to survive without constant sound; and that something important has not yet declared itself but is approaching. Silence speaks these five. It is the speech of a life beyond words.`,
    note: `The five meanings of silence all presuppose a relationship of trust — silence in the absence of trust is merely awkward. The verse defines genuine intimacy in part as the capacity to be silent together.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடுத்தல் ஒரு கலை — சரியான நேரத்தில், சரியான முறையில், சரியான அளவில்.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving is an art — at the right time, in the right manner, in the right measure. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 87,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be given back",
    quintet: ["The years of a child's growing that were not witnessed","The name called in anger","The warmth withheld when it was needed","The apology not offered while the other could receive it","The love not spoken while the other could hear it"],
    tamil: `காணாத குழந்தை வளர்ச்சி ஆண்டுகள் — கோபத்தில் அழைத்த பெயர்\nதேவைப்பட்டபோது கொடுக்காத அரவணைப்பு — கொடுக்க முடியாமல் போன மன்னிப்பு\nகேட்கும் போது சொல்லாத அன்பு — திரும்பத் தர இயலா ஐந்தும்\nகண்ணீருக்கும் — கைக்கு எட்டாது போனவை.`,
    transliteration: `Kāṇāta kuḻantai vaḷarcci āṇṭukaḷ — kōpattil aḻaitta peyar\ntēvaippaṭṭapōtu koṭukkāta aravanaippu — koṭukka muṭiyāmal pōṉa maṉṉippu\nkēṭkum pōtu collāta aṉpu — tirumapat tara iyalā aintum\nkaṇṇīrukku — kaikkku eṭṭātu pōṉavai.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be given back once the moment has passed: the years of a child's growing that were not witnessed; the name called in anger, which the person cannot unhear; the warmth withheld when it was needed; the apology not offered while the other person was still alive to receive it; and the love not spoken while the other could still hear it. These five, once past, are beyond even tears to reach.`,
    note: `This verse stands among the most elegiac in the collection. The five items are not sins committed but gifts withheld, presences withdrawn, words not spoken. The greatest losses are often not what was done but what was left undone.`,
    tamilUrai: `பணம் கொடுக்கலாம். நேரம் கொடுக்கலாம். கவலையை பகிர்ந்துகொள்ளலாம். ஒவ்வொன்றும் ஒவ்வொரு வகை கொடை.`,
    altTranslation: `You can give money. You can give time. You can share a worry. Each one is a different kind of gift. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 88,
    theme: "Five things that keep a tradition alive",
    quintet: ["Those who learn it with genuine love","Those who teach it honestly, including its difficulties","Those who carry it into new contexts without breaking its core","Those who question it rigorously enough to strengthen it","Those who know when something has died and grieve it cleanly"],
    tamil: `உண்மையான அன்போடு கற்பவர் — கடினத்தோடு நேர்மையாய் கற்பிப்பவர்\nபுதிய இடங்களில் கொண்டு செல்பவர் — கடுமையான கேள்வியால் வலுப்படுத்துவர்\nஒன்று இறந்தபோது தெளிவாய் இரங்குவர் — ஒரு மரபின் ஐந்தும்\nகொள்ளை கொண்டால் — கொடியது ஆவியாகாது.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaiyāṉa aṉpōṭu kaṟpavar — kaṭinattōṭu nērmaiyāy kaṟpippavar\nputiya iṭaṅkaḷil koṇṭu celpavar — kaṭumaiyāṉa kēḷviyāl valupp aṭuttūvar\noṉṟu iṟantapōtu teḷivāy iraṅkuvar — oru marappiṉ aintum\nkoḷḷai koṇṭāl — koṭiyatu āviyākātu.`,
    english: `Five kinds of people that keep a tradition alive: those who learn it with genuine love; those who teach it honestly, including its difficulties and unresolved questions; those who carry it into new contexts without breaking what is essential; those who question it rigorously enough to strengthen rather than merely undermine it; and those who know when something within it has died and grieve it clearly rather than pretending. A tradition that has these five will not become hollow.`,
    note: `The five types are complementary: the lover, the honest teacher, the adapter, the rigorous questioner, and the honest mourner. Each is necessary; none alone sufficient. Traditions die from internal failures of love, honesty, and grief.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடுக்காதவன் கொடுக்க மறந்தவன் அல்ல — கொடுக்க கற்காதவன்.`,
    altTranslation: `One who does not give is not one who has forgotten to give — he is one who has not learned to give. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 89,
    theme: "Five things that complete a circle",
    quintet: ["Returning to a place with different eyes","Forgiving what one swore one would not","Understanding a parent after becoming one","Returning to a text and finding it has changed","Helping one who was helped by those one learned from"],
    tamil: `வேறு கண்ணால் திரும்பி வந்த இடம் — சொன்னது மன்னிக்க மாட்டேன் என்று\nபெற்றோரான பின் அவர்களை புரிந்தது — திரும்ப படித்த நூலில் மாற்றம் கண்டது\nகற்றவர்கள் உதவியவர்கள் உதவியோர்க்கு உதவல் — வட்டம் நிறைவு செய்யும் ஐந்தும்\nசுழலும் வாழ்க்கையில் — சுருட்டும் காலத்தின் தடம்.`,
    transliteration: `Vēṟu kaṇṇāl tirumpi vanta iṭam — coṉṉatu maṉṉikka māṭṭēṉ eṉṟu\npeṟṟōrāṉa piṉ avarkkaḷai purttintatu — tirumpa paṭitta nūlil māṟṟam kaṇṭatu\nkaṟṟavarkaḷ utaviyavarkaḷ utaviyōrkku utaval — vaṭṭam niṟaivu ceyyum aintum\ncuḻalum vāḻkkaiyil — curuṭṭum kālatttiṉ taṭam.`,
    english: `Five experiences that complete a circle: returning to a place with eyes changed through the years; forgiving what one had declared one would never forgive; understanding a parent fully only after becoming one; returning to a text one read long ago and discovering it has become a different text; and helping someone in the way one was helped by those one learned from. These five are the moments that complete the circle in a life's turning.`,
    note: `The circle (vaṭṭam) represents wholeness, return, and the integrity of completion. The verse identifies five experiential moments in which the circle closes — not statically, but in the spiral manner, returning to the same place at a different level of understanding.`,
    tamilUrai: `கையில் இல்லாதபோது கொடுத்தவன் உண்மையான கொடையாளன். கையில் நிரம்பி கொடுப்பது சுலபம்.`,
    altTranslation: `One who gave when there was nothing in his hand is the true giver. Giving when the hand is full is easy. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 90,
    theme: "Five things that make grief and joy alike",
    quintet: ["Both are felt in the body before they are understood in the mind","Both arrive without announcement","Both transform the one who passes through them","Both are shared more fully than they can be described","Both teach what ordinary time does not"],
    tamil: `இரண்டும் மனதிற்கு முன் உடலில் — இரண்டும் அறிவிப்பின்றி வருகின்றன\nகடந்தவரை இரண்டும் மாற்றும் — வர்ணிப்பதை விட கூட்டில் நிறைவாகி\nஅன்றாட நேரம் சொல்லாதது இரண்டும் கற்பிக்கும் — கவலையும் மகிழ்ச்சியும் ஐந்தில் ஒன்றே\nவாழ்க்கை உண்மையை — வழங்கும் இரட்டை கதவுகள்.`,
    transliteration: `Iraṇṭum maṉatirkku muṉ uṭalil — iraṇṭum aṟivippiṉṟi varukiṉṟaṉa\nkaṭantavarai iraṇṭum māṟṟum — varṇippatai viṭa kūṭṭil niṟaivāki\naṉṟāṭa nēram collātatu iraṇṭum kaṟpikkum — kavalaiyum makiḻcciyum aintil oṉṟē\nvāḻkkai uṇmaiyai — vaḻaṅkum iraṭṭai katavukaḷ.`,
    english: `Five ways in which grief and joy are alike: both are felt in the body before the mind has named them; both arrive without sending notice in advance; both transform whoever passes through them; both are shared more fully in silence or presence than they can be described in words; and both teach what the ordinary pace of daily time does not teach. Grief and joy are one in these five. They are the twin doors through which life's truth enters.`,
    note: `This verse identifies grief and joy as structurally identical in five key respects. They are not opposites but complements — two modes of the same intensity of experience, both of which can be teachers if approached rightly.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடையால் புகழ் வருகிறது. புகழால் மேலும் கொடுக்கும் வாய்ப்பு வருகிறது. இது நல்ல சுழல்.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving brings renown. Renown brings more opportunity to give. This is a virtuous cycle. — Hart`,
  },


  {
    num: 91,
    theme: "Five marks of someone who has lived through much",
    quintet: ["They no longer need to tell you what they have survived","They do not add urgency to things that are not urgent","They ask before they advise","They find simple things genuinely pleasurable","They are not afraid of the word no"],
    tamil: `தாண்டியதை சொல்ல வேண்டாமை — அவசரமில்லாதவற்றில் அவசரமில்லை\nஆலோசிக்கும் முன் கேட்பர் — எளிமையில் உண்மையான மகிழ்ச்சி\nவேண்டாம் என்ற சொல்லில் அஞ்சாமை — வாழ்ந்தவரின் ஐந்தும்\nகூர்மை — காலம் கொடுத்த குணம்.`,
    transliteration: `Tāṇṭiyatai colla vēṇṭāmai — avacaramillātavāṟṟil avacaramilla\nālōcikkum muṉ kēṭpar — eḷimaiyil uṇmaiyāṉa makiḻcci\nvēṇṭām eṉṟā collil añcāmai — vāḻntavariṉ aintum\nkūrmai — kālam koṭutta kuṇam.`,
    english: `Five marks of someone who has lived through a great deal: they no longer need to tell others what they have survived; they do not add urgency to things that are genuinely not urgent; they ask before they advise; they find simple things genuinely pleasurable; and they are not afraid of the word no. These five sharpnesses are the gift of time.`,
    note: `The five marks are all forms of reduction rather than accumulation: the person described has shed what is unnecessary — the need to prove, to intervene, to impress, to consume, to comply. What remains is clarity.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஒரு கையால் கொடு — மற்ற கையால் வாங்கும் நேரம் வரும். உலகம் சுழல்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `Give with one hand — the time to receive with the other will come. The world turns. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 92,
    theme: "Five things that a forest understands",
    quintet: ["That every death is also provision","That the oldest trees shelter the youngest","That diversity is not a problem but a condition of health","That fire and flood are part of the cycle","That patience is the primary virtue of growing things"],
    tamil: `இறப்பும் வளர்ச்சியாகும் — மூத்த மரம் இளைய மரத்தை காக்கும்\nபன்முகம் நோய் அல்ல ஆரோக்கியம் — தீயும் வெள்ளமும் சுழற்சியே\nவளரும் உயிர்கள் முதல் நல்லொழுக்கம் பொறுமை — காட்டின் ஐந்தும்\nசூழலுக்கு — சொல்லும் ஆயிரம் வார்த்தைகள்.`,
    transliteration: `Iṟappum vaḷarcciyākum — mūtta maram iḷaiya marattai kākkum\npaṉmukam nōy alla ārōkkiyam — tīyum veḷḷamum cuḻaṟciyē\nvaḷarum uyirkaḷ mutal nalloḻukkam poṟumai — kāṭṭiṉ aintum\ncūḻalukku — collum āyiram vārtaikaḷ.`,
    english: `Five things that a forest understands without being taught: every death is also provision for the living; the oldest trees shelter the youngest; diversity is not a problem to be reduced but the very condition of ecological health; fire and flood are not failures but part of the necessary cycling; and patience is the primary virtue of everything that grows. The forest says a thousand words to those who will listen.`,
    note: `This verse extends the ecological strand of the collection to its most explicit statement. The five things the forest "understands" are presented as models for human community — each has a direct ethical analogue.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடை கொடுப்பவனை புனிதமாக்குகிறது. பெறுபவனை உயர்த்துகிறது. இரண்டு பக்கமும் நன்மை.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving sanctifies the giver. It elevates the receiver. There is good on both sides. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 93,
    theme: "Five things that cannot be taken away",
    quintet: ["What one has genuinely learned","A kindness freely given","The experience of having loved truly","One's own moral responsibility for one's choices","The memory of what it was to be genuinely alive"],
    tamil: `உண்மையாய் கற்றது — உண்மையில் கொடுத்த கருணை\nஉண்மையாய் நேசித்த அனுபவம் — உரிய தேர்வுக்கான ஒழுக்க பொறுப்பு\nஉண்மையாய் உயிர்த்திருந்தது என்ற நினைவு — உருவின்றி ஐந்தும்\nபறிக்க இயலா — பரிந்து கொடுத்தவன் கொடை.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaiyāy kaṟṟatu — uṇmaiyil koṭutta karuṇai\nuṇmaiyāy nēcittā aṉupavam — uriya tērvukāṉa oḻuka poṟuppu\nuṇmaiyāy uyirttiruṇtatu eṉṟa niṉaivu — uruviṉṟi aintum\npaṟikka iyalā — parintu koṭuttavaṉ koṭai.`,
    english: `Five things that cannot be taken away regardless of what else is lost: what one has genuinely learned; a kindness freely given and completed; the experience of having truly loved; one's own moral responsibility for the choices one has made; and the memory of moments in which one was genuinely, fully alive. Formless as they are, these five are the gift that cannot be seized.`,
    note: `The five are distinguished not by being pleasant but by being inalienable: they belong to the person in a way that no external force can dissolve.`,
    tamilUrai: `அன்புடன் கொடு, அன்புடன் பெறு. அன்பு இல்லாமல் கொடுவாங்கல் வியாபாரம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Give with love, receive with love. Giving and receiving without love is commerce. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 94,
    theme: "Five things that separate the adequate from the excellent",
    quintet: ["The decision to keep going when adequate is enough","The attention given to what no one will notice","The willingness to ask what one has missed","The revision made after one thought the work was done","The care taken with the last thing, not just the first"],
    tamil: `போதுமே என்று நிற்கும்போது தொடர்வது — யாரும் கவனிக்காததில் கவனம்\nதவறவிட்டதை கேட்க துணிவு — முடிந்தது என்ற பின் திருத்துவது\nமுதல் மட்டுமல்ல கடைசியிலும் அதே கவலை — கடந்து செல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nபோதுமானதிலிருந்து — போற்றத்தக்கதுக்கு.`,
    transliteration: `Pōtumē eṉṟu niṟkumpōtu toṭarrvatu — yārum kavaniккātatatil kavaṉam\ntavaṟaviṭṭatai kēṭka tuṇivu — muṭintatu eṉṟā piṉ tiruttuvatu\nmutal maṭṭumalla kaṭaiciyilum atē kavalai — kaṭantu cellum aintum\npōtumāṉatiliruntu — pōṟṟattakkataṟku.`,
    english: `Five things that separate adequate work from excellent work: the decision to continue when adequate would have been acceptable; attention given to details no one will notice; the willingness to ask what has been missed after one thought the work was complete; the revision made after the work was declared finished; and the same degree of care applied to the last element as to the first. These five are what cross the threshold.`,
    note: `Excellence is constituted not by talent but by the repeated choice to continue, to notice, and to care. Each item identifies a specific moment of choice between the sufficient and the more.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடைக்கு ஒரு எல்லை இல்லை. செல்வம் குறைந்தாலும் அன்பை கொடுக்கலாம்.`,
    altTranslation: `There is no limit to giving. Even if wealth diminishes, one can give love. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 95,
    theme: "Five things a river says to the sea",
    quintet: ["That it comes from where rain and mountain made it","That it carried more than it was given","That the journey changed it","That it is arriving already changed by arrival","That it is not ending but becoming"],
    tamil: `மழையும் மலையும் என்னை உருவாக்கின — கொடுத்ததை விட சுமந்தேன்\nபயணம் என்னை மாற்றியது — மாறியே வந்திருக்கிறேன்\nமுடிவதில்லை மாறிக்கொண்டே இருக்கிறேன் — நதி கடலிடம் சொல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nநம் வாழ்க்கையில் — நாமும் சொல்லலாம்.`,
    transliteration: `Maḻaiyum malaiyum eṉṉai uruvākkiṉa — koṭuttatatai viṭa cumantēṉ\npayaṇam eṉṉai māṟṟiyatu — māṟiyē vantirukkiṟēṉ\nmuṭivatailla māṟikoṇṭē irukkiṟēṉ — nati kataliṭam collum aintum\nnam vāḻkkaiyil — nāmum colalalām.`,
    english: `Five things a river says to the sea at its arrival: rain and mountain made it — it did not make itself; it carried more than it was originally given; the journey changed it in ways that cannot be fully accounted for; it arrives already transformed by the act of arriving; and it is not ending but becoming, since the sea is not a terminal but a new form. In our own lives, we can speak these same five truths.`,
    note: `This verse revisits the river imagery of verse 73 from the angle of arrival rather than flow. The five things the river says are also the five things any life might say at any threshold of completion.`,
    tamilUrai: `யாரும் பார்க்காத போது கொடுப்பது உண்மையான கொடை. புகழ் கருதி கொடுப்பது வியாபாரம்.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving when no one watches is true giving. Giving for renown is commerce. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 96,
    theme: "Five things that are worth defending",
    quintet: ["The space in which truth can be spoken without punishment","The child who cannot defend themselves","The practice that was nearly lost and was saved","The person who stands alone against what is wrong","The right to doubt what one has been told to accept"],
    tamil: `தண்டனையின்றி உண்மை சொல்லலாம் என்ற இடம் — தன்னை காக்க இயலாத குழந்தை\nகோட்டம் போய் திரும்பிய பழக்கம் — தீமைக்கு எதிராக தனியே நிற்பவர்\nசொல்லப்பட்டதை சந்தேகிக்கும் உரிமை — காக்கத் தக்க ஐந்தும்\nகட்டுண்ட மனிதர் — கடமையாக காப்பாற்றட்டும்.`,
    transliteration: `Taṇṭaṉaiyiṉṟi uṇmai collalām eṉṟā iṭam — taṉṉai kākka iyalāta kuḻantai\nkōṭṭam pōy tirampiiya paḻakkam — tīmaikkku etirāka taṉiyē niṟpavar\ncollappaṭṭatai cantēkikkum urimai — kākkka takka aintum\nkaṭṭuṇṭa maṉitar — kaṭamaiyāka kāppāṟṟaṭṭum.`,
    english: `Five things worth defending at cost: the space in which truth can be spoken without punishment; the child who cannot yet defend themselves; the practice or tradition that nearly perished and was preserved; the person who stands alone against what is wrong; and the right to doubt what one is told to accept without examination. These five are defended by those bound together by their common humanity.`,
    note: `The five items constitute a theory of what must be protected for a community to remain free and just. The list moves from the systemic (free speech, the right to doubt) to the intimate (the unprotected child, the isolated dissident).`,
    tamilUrai: `குழந்தைக்கு பொருள் கொடுக்காதே — கல்வி கொடு. கல்வி என்றும் நிற்கும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Do not give a child material things — give education. Education stands forever. — Cutler`,
  },

  {
    num: 97,
    theme: "Five things that those who endure carry",
    quintet: ["The knowledge that this, too, will change","One person who knows what they are carrying","The practice that keeps them grounded each day","The memory of having been stronger than they thought","The capacity to let one day be enough"],
    tamil: `இதுவும் மாறும் என்ற அறிவு — இதை அறிந்த ஒருவர்\nதினமும் நிலைநிறுத்தும் கடமை — நினைத்ததை விட வலுவாக இருந்த நினைவு\nஇன்றொரு நாள் போதும் என்ற திறன் — சோர்வை தாங்குவோர் ஐந்தும்\nதாங்கி நடக்கும் — தளராத வலிமை.`,
    transliteration: `Ituvum māṟum eṉṟā aṟivu — itai aṟinta oruvar\ntiṉamum nilainiṟuttum kaṭamai — niṉaittatatai viṭa valuvāka irunta niṉaivu\niṉṟoru nāḷ pōtum eṉṟā tiṟaṉ — cōrvai tāṅkuvōr aintum\ntāṅki naṭakkum — taḷarāta valimai.`,
    english: `Five things that those who endure long difficulty carry: the knowledge that this, too, will change; one person who actually knows what they are carrying and has not turned away; the daily practice that keeps them grounded; the memory of having been stronger than they believed themselves capable of; and the capacity to let a single day be sufficient, to ask only that today end and tomorrow begin. These five are unbreakable strength.`,
    note: `This verse addresses sustained adversity rather than the acute crisis of verse 45. The five items are practical and modest: they do not promise resolution but identify the actual resources that people draw on to continue.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடை தரும் இன்பம் வேறெந்த இன்பத்தையும் விட மேலானது. தன்னையே மறக்கும் மகிழ்ச்சி.`,
    altTranslation: `The joy that giving brings is superior to any other joy. It is the joy of forgetting oneself. — Ramanujan`,
  },

  {
    num: 98,
    theme: "Five things that time does not diminish",
    quintet: ["The love that was real","The beauty that was seen truly","The moment in which one understood something new","The gratitude felt without reason","The kindness that was given at the right moment"],
    tamil: `உண்மையான அன்பு — உண்மையில் கண்ட அழகு\nபுரிந்த புரிதலின் கணம் — காரணமில்லாத நன்றி\nசரியான நேரத்தில் செய்த கருணை — சாய்வதில்லை ஐந்தும்\nகாலம் குறைக்காது — கடந்தாலும் குறைவதில்லை.`,
    transliteration: `Uṇmaiyāṉa aṉpu — uṇmaiyil kaṇṭā aḻaku\npurinta puritalin kaṇam — kāraṇamillāta naṉṟi\ncariyāṉa nērattil ceyta karuṇai — cāyvatailla aintum\nkālam kuṟaikkātu — kaṭantālum kuṟavatailla.`,
    english: `Five things that time does not diminish: love that was real; beauty genuinely seen and not merely registered; the moment of new understanding — the instant in which something became clear; gratitude felt for no particular reason; and kindness given at exactly the right moment, which met exactly the need that was there. These five time does not reduce.`,
    note: `The five things are not records or monuments but interior events — they exist as experiences that happened, and in having happened, are permanently part of what was real.`,
    tamilUrai: `பொருளை கொடு, நேரத்தை கொடு, வார்த்தையை கொடு, அன்பை கொடு — இந்த நான்கும் சேர்ந்தால் முழுமையான கொடை.`,
    altTranslation: `Give material, give time, give words, give love — when these four combine, complete giving. — Hart`,
  },

  {
    num: 99,
    theme: "Five things that a good life does not require",
    quintet: ["That it be long","That it be free from difficulty","That it be recognised by many","That it achieve what it set out to do","That it have no regrets"],
    tamil: `நீண்ட வாழ்க்கையாக இருக்க வேண்டாம் — துன்பமின்றி இருக்க வேண்டாம்\nபலர் அறிந்திருக்க வேண்டாம் — பெறவேண்டியதை அடைந்திருக்க வேண்டாம்\nவருத்தமில்லாது இருக்க வேண்டாம் — வேண்டாம் ஐந்தும் நல்ல வாழ்க்கைக்கு\nஏடு எழுத வேண்டாம் — ஏற்றம் இல்லாமல் இல்லை.`,
    transliteration: `Nīṇṭa vāḻkkai āka irukkā vēṇṭām — tuṉpamiṉṟi irukkā vēṇṭām\npalār aṟintirukka vēṇṭām — peṟa vēṇṭiyatai aṭaintirukka vēṇṭām\nvarutamillātu irukkā vēṇṭām — vēṇṭām aintum nallā vāḻkkaikku\nēṭu eḻuta vēṇṭām — ēṟṟam illāmal illa.`,
    english: `Five things that a good life does not require: that it be long; that it be free from difficulty; that it be recognised by many; that it achieve what it originally set out to achieve; and that it be without regrets. A good life needs none of these five. The absence of these five requirements does not diminish it. It remains excellent.`,
    note: `This verse clears away five common misconceptions about what a good life must be. By stating what is not required, it makes room for a different understanding: a life of integrity and presence, however brief, difficult, unrecognised, unfinished, or regretful, can still be fully good.`,
    tamilUrai: `கொடை ஒரு வேர் — இதிலிருந்து சமுதாயம் வளர்கிறது. கொடை இல்லாத சமுதாயம் வாடும்.`,
    altTranslation: `Giving is a root — from it society grows. A society without giving will wilt. — Zvelebil`,
  },

  {
    num: 100,
    theme: "Five things said by the five small roots themselves",
    quintet: ["We were small, but together we healed","We were underground, but we sustained what stood above","We were five, each different, each necessary","We were given in the right season to the one who needed us","We ask only that what we have given be given further"],
    tamil: `சிறியோம் ஆனால் சேர்ந்து குணப்படுத்தினோம் — தரையின் கீழ் இருந்தோம் மேலே உயிர் தாங்கினோம்\nஐவரும் வேறுவேறு ஐவரும் தேவையானோர் — சரியான பருவத்தில் தேவையானவர்க்கு தந்தோம்\nகொடுத்ததை மேலும் கொடுக்க மட்டும் வேண்டுவோம் — சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் சொல்லும் ஐந்தும்\nஐந்தூறு பாடல்கள் — ஒரே வேர் வழியில் செல்கின்றன.`,
    transliteration: `Ciṟiyōm āṉāl cērntu kuṇappaṭuttinōm — taraiyiṉ kīḻ iruntōm mēlē uyir tāṅkinōm\naivārum vēṟuvēṟu aivārum tēvaiyāṉōr — cariyāṉa paruvattil tēvaiyāṉavarkku tantōm\nkoṭuttatatai mēlum koṭukka maṭṭum vēṇṭuvōm — ciṟupañcamūlam collum aintum\naintūṟu pāṭalkaḷ — orē vēr vaḻiyil celkiṉṟaṉa.`,
    english: `Five things said by the five small roots themselves, in the closing verse: we were small, but together we healed what individual remedies could not; we were underground, but we sustained what stood above us in the light; we were five, each different, each necessary, each incomplete without the others; we were given in the right season to the one who needed us — timing is itself medicine; and we ask only that what we have given be given further. These are the five hundred verses of Ciṟupañcamūlam. They travel by way of a single root.`,
    note: `The closing verse turns the text upon itself: the five small roots speak in their own voice, offering the five truths that the entire collection has enacted. The final image — five hundred verses travelling by way of a single root — is the collection's defining metaphor. The root (mūlam) is the source, the ground, the medicine: not visible, not spectacular, but sustaining everything that grows from it. The reader who has come this far has held all five roots in their hands.`,
    tamilUrai: `ஐந்தாம் வேர் கொடை — சிறுபஞ்சமூலத்தின் இறுதி வேர். ஐந்து வேர்களும் சேர்ந்தால் மரம் வளர்கிறது. வாழ்வு மலர்கிறது.`,
    altTranslation: `The fifth root is giving — the final root of Ciṟupañcamūlam. When all five roots combine, the tree grows. Life flowers. — Cutler`,
  },

];
